/* 生成的文件，不要手改：node tools/group-build.mjs 从 tools/group/ 生成（inputs 9564f55117a21565，公式由 katex@0.16.11 转成 MathML）。 */
window.MOLASTRIA_GROUP = {"index":{"title":"Group Theory","sub":"The mathematics of symmetry: from molecular point groups, character tables and spectroscopic selection rules to crystals, bands, angular momentum and symmetry breaking","eyebrow":"MOLASTRIA · GROUP THEORY","h1":"Group Theory","question":"How can you say, just from what a molecule looks like and without solving a single equation, how many infrared bands it has, which orbitals can bond and how its d orbitals split?","lead":"Symmetry operations (rotations, reflections, inversion, translations) together form a <b>group</b>. The Hamiltonian is unchanged by them, so its eigenstates must be sorted by the group’s <b>irreducible representations</b>: where degeneracies come from, which integrals must vanish and which transitions are forbidden are all decided by the group, independent of the particular force field, basis set or approximation. That is why chemists and physicists use group theory every day. This section has twelve chapters, from “what is a group” to point groups and the flow chart for finding them, representations and character tables and the reduction formula, then applications to molecular orbitals, vibrational spectra, ligand fields, crystals, angular momentum and bands, and finally symmetry breaking and where group theory is going today. Every chapter has a demo computed live in your browser: the point-group flow chart is clickable, and the program finds every symmetry element from the atomic coordinates before walking step by step to the answer.","scope":"<b>How to read the labels.</b> Every chapter and every key point carries a status: history, theorem (proved rigorously in mathematics), established by experiment, approximation (valid under the stated conditions), frontier and open. Group theory itself is almost all theorems; when it is applied to particular molecules and materials the numbers often come from approximate models, and those are labelled separately. Demos carry one of three labels: exact (computed live from the definitions, no approximation), toy model (a minimal model built to show one thing) and data (numbers from the literature).","levels":{"history":["History","Documented history and facts about the literature"],"proved":["Theorem","Proved rigorously in mathematics"],"tested":["Established","Supported by several independent experiments, and in everyday use"],"approx":["Approximation","Holds under the stated conditions and fails outside them"],"frontier":["Frontier","Developing fast; conclusions and best methods are still changing"],"open":["Open","Not yet proved or explained by anyone"]},"demoKinds":{"exact":["Exact","Computed live from the definitions, with no approximation (the method is described under each demo)"],"approx":["Approximate model","The symmetry part is exact; the actual numbers come from an approximate model (described under the demo)"],"toy":["Toy model","A minimal model built to show one thing clearly; its parameters are only roughly realistic"]},"groups":[{"title":"Groups and symmetry","ids":["what","pointgroups"]},{"title":"Representation theory: the toolkit","ids":["reps","reduce"]},{"title":"Applied to molecules","ids":["orbitals","vibrations","ligand"]},{"title":"Crystals and continuous groups","ids":["crystals","lie","angular","bands"]},{"title":"Breaking and frontiers","ids":["beyond"]}],"chapters":[{"id":"what","n":"01","title":"What a group is: the rules that symmetry operations obey","summary":"Operations that leave something looking the same: doing two in a row gives another, each can be undone, and doing nothing counts as one. Those four facts are the definition of a group. This chapter covers the group axioms, multiplication tables, order, subgroups and cosets (Lagrange’s theorem), conjugacy classes, and why “symmetry” is so useful in physics and chemistry.","level":"proved","demos":["cayley"]},{"id":"pointgroups","n":"02","title":"Point groups: molecular symmetry and the flow chart","summary":"A molecule’s symmetry operations leave at least one point fixed, hence “point group”. This chapter covers the five kinds of symmetry element (E, Cₙ, σ, i, Sₙ), Schoenflies notation, and the flow chart for finding a point group: a chain of yes/no questions from the special groups down to Cₙ, ⊥C₂, σh and σv. The demo is a clickable chart with a 3D molecule; worked examples are trans-1-bromo-2-chloroethylene (Cs) and dichloromethane (C2v).","level":"proved","demos":["flow","xyz"]},{"id":"reps","n":"03","title":"Representations and character tables","summary":"Write each symmetry operation as a matrix whose multiplication copies the group’s: that is a representation. Representations split into pieces that cannot be split further, the irreducible representations, whose traces (characters) are laid out in a character table. This chapter covers representations, equivalence and reducibility, the orthogonality theorem, how to read a character table, Mulliken notation, and direct products and when an integral must vanish.","level":"proved","demos":["chartable"]},{"id":"reduce","n":"04","title":"The reduction formula and projection operators: sorting a set of objects by symmetry","summary":"With a character table, any set of bonds, orbitals or displacements can be sorted by symmetry in three steps: count the character for each class, use the reduction formula to find how often each irrep occurs, then build symmetry-adapted linear combinations (SALCs) with projection operators. This chapter runs the procedure on methane’s four bonds, ammonia’s three hydrogens, benzene’s π system and an octahedron’s six bonds.","level":"proved","demos":["reduce"]},{"id":"orbitals","n":"05","title":"Molecular orbitals: symmetry fixes the shape of the answer first","summary":"Molecular orbitals must be sorted by the irreducible representations of the point group, and only atomic orbitals of the same symmetry can mix. This chapter shows how water’s orbital diagram is assembled from symmetry, why the orbitals of cyclic conjugated molecules are known without solving anything (the Frost circle and the 4N + 2 rule), and the Woodward–Hoffmann rules for conservation of orbital symmetry along a reaction.","level":"tested","demos":["huckel"]},{"id":"vibrations","n":"06","title":"Vibrational spectra: counting atoms that stay put","summary":"An N-atom molecule has 3N − 6 vibrations. Without knowing any force constant, group theory says which symmetry species they fall into, which appear in the infrared, which in Raman and which in neither. This chapter covers how to compute Γ3N and Γvib, the IR and Raman selection rules and the mutual-exclusion rule, then actually solves for the normal modes and watches symmetry appear in the answer by itself.","level":"tested","demos":["vib","modes"]},{"id":"ligand","n":"07","title":"Ligand fields: why and how d orbitals split","summary":"The five d orbitals of a free atom are degenerate; put the atom inside an octahedron, tetrahedron or square of ligands and they split into sets according to the irreps of the point group. How many sets, and how many orbitals in each, is fixed by symmetry (a theorem); which is higher and by how much needs a model (an approximation). This chapter covers Bethe’s crystal field, descent in symmetry (correlation tables), high and low spin, the Jahn–Teller effect, Tanabe–Sugano diagrams and the angular overlap model.","level":"approx","demos":["aom"]},{"id":"crystals","n":"08","title":"Crystals: translations, space groups and why there are no five-fold axes","summary":"A crystal’s symmetry has one thing a molecule’s lacks: translations. Combined with rotations and reflections they produce screw axes and glide planes, and there are only 230 space groups; in the plane, only 17 wallpaper groups. Translations also restrict rotations to 1-, 2-, 3-, 4- and 6-fold. This chapter covers lattices, crystal systems, point and space groups, Hermann–Mauguin symbols, the crystallographic restriction and quasicrystals, and how programs determine space groups today.","level":"proved","demos":["wallpaper","restrict"]},{"id":"lie","n":"09","title":"Lie groups: continuous symmetry, the rotation group and spin","summary":"Rotations can be by any angle; groups like that are Lie groups. Almost everything about a Lie group is written in its “infinitesimal generators” and their commutation relations (its Lie algebra). This chapter covers SO(3) and its generators, where the angular-momentum commutation relations come from, how SU(2) double-covers SO(3) (a spin ½ changes sign after 360°), the classification of simple Lie algebras, and SU(3) and the eightfold way.","level":"proved","demos":["su2"]},{"id":"angular","n":"10","title":"Angular momentum: representations of the rotation group, Clebsch–Gordan coefficients and the Wigner–Eckart theorem","summary":"Irreps of the rotation group are labelled by j, with dimension 2j + 1 and spherical harmonics as a basis. Adding two angular momenta is taking the direct product of two representations, and Clebsch–Gordan coefficients are the change of basis that splits it. The Wigner–Eckart theorem then splits every matrix element into a “geometric” and a “physical” part, which is where all of atomic spectroscopy’s selection rules come from. The tensor products inside equivariant neural networks use the same mathematics.","level":"proved","demos":["cg"]},{"id":"bands","n":"11","title":"Bands: the translation group, the Brillouin zone and protected degeneracies","summary":"The translation group is abelian, with irreps labelled by a wavevector k: hence Bloch’s theorem and bands. The point group then leaves a “little group” at each k, which decides which bands must be degenerate and how bands are labelled at high-symmetry points. Time reversal and space-group representations can even decide band topology directly. This chapter runs from Bloch’s theorem to topological quantum chemistry.","level":"proved","demos":["bands"]},{"id":"beyond","n":"12","title":"Symmetry breaking and group theory today","summary":"Symmetric equations, an asymmetric answer: spontaneous symmetry breaking explains magnets, superconductors, liquid crystals and the masses of particles. Nature can also break a symmetry outright (parity violation). This chapter covers Landau theory, Goldstone’s theorem and the Higgs mechanism, Wigner’s definition of a “particle” through the Poincaré group, and two of group theory’s frontiers today: equivariant neural networks and monstrous moonshine.","level":"frontier","demos":["landau"]}],"milestones":[{"year":"1854","text":"Cayley defines a group abstractly for the first time: a set of symbols and a multiplication table, whatever they stand for <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-cayley1854\" data-ref=\"cayley1854\">Cayley 1854</a>]</span> (<a href=\"#c-what\" data-open=\"what\">01 · What a group is: the rules that symmetry operations obey</a>)."},{"year":"1888","text":"Killing begins classifying simple Lie algebras, later completed by Cartan: four infinite families plus five exceptions <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-killing1888\" data-ref=\"killing1888\">Killing 1888</a>]</span> (<a href=\"#c-lie\" data-open=\"lie\">09 · Lie groups: continuous symmetry, the rotation group and spin</a>)."},{"year":"1918","text":"Noether’s theorem: every continuous symmetry gives a conserved quantity <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-noether1918\" data-ref=\"noether1918\">Noether 1918</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1924","text":"Pólya lists the 17 wallpaper groups of the plane <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-polya1924\" data-ref=\"polya1924\">Pólya 1924</a>]</span> (<a href=\"#c-crystals\" data-open=\"crystals\">08 · Crystals: translations, space groups and why there are no five-fold axes</a>)."},{"year":"1925","text":"The Laporte rule: electric-dipole transitions in atoms must change parity <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-laporte1925\" data-ref=\"laporte1925\">Laporte &amp; Meggers 1925</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1929","text":"Bethe uses point groups to work out how atomic levels split in a crystal field <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bethe1929\" data-ref=\"bethe1929\">Bethe 1929</a>]</span>; Bloch’s theorem the same year <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bloch1929\" data-ref=\"bloch1929\">Bloch 1929</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1931","text":"Hückel’s π-electron theory, the starting point of the 4N + 2 rule <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-huckel1931\" data-ref=\"huckel1931\">Hückel 1931</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1936","text":"Bouckaert, Smoluchowski and Wigner label bands by the “little groups” of the Brillouin zone <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bsw1936\" data-ref=\"bsw1936\">Bouckaert et al. 1936</a>]</span> (<a href=\"#c-bands\" data-open=\"bands\">11 · Bands: the translation group, the Brillouin zone and protected degeneracies</a>)."},{"year":"1937","text":"The Jahn–Teller theorem: a degenerate electronic state makes a non-linear molecule distort itself <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-jahnteller1937\" data-ref=\"jahnteller1937\">Jahn &amp; Teller 1937</a>]</span> (<a href=\"#c-ligand\" data-open=\"ligand\">07 · Ligand fields: why and how d orbitals split</a>)."},{"year":"1939","text":"Wigner classifies particles by irreducible representations of the Poincaré group: a particle is a mass and a spin <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-wigner1939\" data-ref=\"wigner1939\">Wigner 1939</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1955","text":"Mulliken’s report standardises the notation A, B, E, T, g, u for character tables and spectra <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-mulliken1955\" data-ref=\"mulliken1955\">Mulliken 1955</a>]</span> (<a href=\"#c-reps\" data-open=\"reps\">03 · Representations and character tables</a>)."},{"year":"1957","text":"Wu’s experiment confirms that parity is not conserved in the weak interaction <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-wu1957\" data-ref=\"wu1957\">Wu et al. 1957</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1961","text":"The eightfold way: Gell-Mann and Ne’eman classify hadrons with SU(3) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-neeman1961\" data-ref=\"neeman1961\">Ne’eman 1961</a>; <a href=\"#ref-gellmann1962\" data-ref=\"gellmann1962\">Gell-Mann 1962</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1964","text":"Englert–Brout and Higgs: spontaneous breaking of a gauge symmetry gives gauge bosons mass <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-englert1964\" data-ref=\"englert1964\">Englert &amp; Brout 1964</a>; <a href=\"#ref-higgs1964\" data-ref=\"higgs1964\">Higgs 1964</a>]</span> (<a href=\"#c-beyond\" data-open=\"beyond\">12 · Symmetry breaking and group theory today</a>)."},{"year":"1965","text":"The Woodward–Hoffmann rules: orbital symmetry is conserved along a reaction <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-woodward1965\" data-ref=\"woodward1965\">Woodward &amp; Hoffmann 1965</a>]</span> (<a href=\"#c-orbitals\" data-open=\"orbitals\">05 · Molecular orbitals: symmetry fixes the shape of the answer first</a>)."},{"year":"1975","text":"Neutron interferometry measures the minus sign of a spin ½ turned by 360° <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-rauch1975\" data-ref=\"rauch1975\">Rauch et al. 1975</a>; <a href=\"#ref-werner1975\" data-ref=\"werner1975\">Werner et al. 1975</a>]</span> (<a href=\"#c-lie\" data-open=\"lie\">09 · Lie groups: continuous symmetry, the rotation group and spin</a>)."},{"year":"1984","text":"Shechtman discovers quasicrystals with five-fold (icosahedral) diffraction symmetry <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-shechtman1984\" data-ref=\"shechtman1984\">Shechtman et al. 1984</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2017","text":"Topological quantum chemistry and symmetry indicators: space-group representations decide band topology directly <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bradlyn2017\" data-ref=\"bradlyn2017\">Bradlyn et al. 2017</a>; <a href=\"#ref-po2017\" data-ref=\"po2017\">Po et al. 2017</a>]</span>; in 2019 three teams used them to screen thousands of topological materials."},{"year":"2022","text":"NequIP, an E(3)-equivariant neural-network interatomic potential: group representations written into the network’s structure <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-batzner2022\" data-ref=\"batzner2022\">Batzner et al. 2022</a>]</span> (<a href=\"#c-beyond\" data-open=\"beyond\">12 · Symmetry breaking and group theory today</a>)."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which of these is <b>not</b> a group under ordinary multiplication?","opts":["{1, −1}","{1, i, −1, −i}","All non-zero real numbers","All integers"],"a":3,"why":"The inverse of 2, which is 1/2, is not an integer. The integers form a group under addition.","ch":"what"},{"q":"Can a group of order 6 have a subgroup of order 4?","opts":["Yes","No: the order of a subgroup divides the order of the group","Only if the group is abelian","It depends on the group"],"a":1,"why":"Lagrange’s theorem: cosets cut the group into equal blocks, and 4 does not divide 6.","ch":"what"},{"q":"What is the point group of dichloromethane CH₂Cl₂?","opts":["T_d","C₃ᵥ","C₂ᵥ","D₂d"],"a":2,"why":"There is a single C₂ (bisecting the HCH and ClCCl angles) and two mirrors each containing one like pair; no ⊥C₂ and no σh.","ch":"pointgroups"},{"q":"A point group has 4 classes and order 8. What are the dimensions of its irreducible representations?","opts":["1, 1, 1, 1","1, 1, 1, √5","No such group exists","1, 1, 2, 2"],"a":2,"why":"There are as many irreps as classes, 4, and their squared dimensions sum to 8; but four positive squares can only add up to 4, 7, 10, 13, …, never 8 (and one dimension must be 1). So no group of order 8 has exactly 4 classes: the abelian ones have 8, D₄ and Q₈ have 5 (1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 4 = 8).","ch":"reps"},{"q":"If the reduction formula gives a non-integer nᵢ, what does it most likely mean?","opts":["The molecule has no symmetry","The characters of the reducible representation were counted wrongly","A larger basis set is needed","That irrep occurs half a time"],"a":1,"why":"nᵢ must be a non-negative integer, which is itself a check on the counting.","ch":"reduce"},{"q":"Why are benzene’s 6 π electrons especially stable?","opts":["6 is even","C₆ symmetry splits the orbitals into 1 + 2 + 2 + 1, and 6 electrons exactly fill the three bonding ones","The carbons are sp² hybridised","Benzene is planar"],"a":1,"why":"4N + 2 electrons close a shell; 4N would put one electron in each of the top degenerate pair (<a href=\"#c-orbitals\" data-open=\"orbitals\">05 · Molecular orbitals: symmetry fixes the shape of the answer first</a>).","ch":"orbitals"},{"q":"CO₂ has a centre of symmetry. Is its symmetric stretch visible in the infrared?","opts":["Yes, the strongest band","No: it is g, IR sees only u; it appears in Raman","Only at high temperature","It depends on concentration"],"a":1,"why":"With a centre of symmetry x, y, z are all u and quadratic functions all g: IR and Raman are mutually exclusive.","ch":"vibrations"},{"q":"Into which two sets do the d orbitals of an octahedral complex split?","opts":["a₁g and t₁u","t₂g (xy, xz, yz) and e_g (z², x² − y²)","e and t₂, with e below","All five stay degenerate"],"a":1,"why":"The d orbitals reduce to T₂g + E_g in Oh; e_g, pointing at the ligands, is pushed up (<a href=\"#c-ligand\" data-open=\"ligand\">07 · Ligand fields: why and how d orbitals split</a>).","ch":"ligand"},{"q":"Why can a periodic crystal not have a five-fold rotation axis?","opts":["Because pentagons cannot tile the plane, and the rotation’s trace in the lattice basis, 2cos 72°, is not an integer","Because atoms are too big","It actually can","Because 5 is odd"],"a":0,"why":"The crystallographic restriction: only 1-, 2-, 3-, 4- and 6-fold axes are allowed. Quasicrystals have five-fold symmetry but no translational period.","ch":"crystals"},{"q":"After a spin-½ particle is rotated by 360°, what becomes of its wavefunction?","opts":["Unchanged","Multiplied by −1","Multiplied by i","A different spin state"],"a":1,"why":"In SU(2), U(2π) = −1, and it takes 720° to come back; neutron interferometry measured the sign.","ch":"lie"},{"q":"Adding two angular momenta with j = 1, which values can the total J take?","opts":["Only 2","0, 1, 2","1, 2","0, 2"],"a":1,"why":"D¹ ⊗ D¹ = D⁰ ⊕ D¹ ⊕ D², with dimensions 3 × 3 = 1 + 3 + 5.","ch":"angular"},{"q":"What does “spontaneous symmetry breaking” mean?","opts":["The equations themselves are not symmetric","The equations are symmetric but the ground state is not, and there is a family of equivalent ground states","An external force breaks the symmetry","Symmetry fades away over time"],"a":1,"why":"For example a ferromagnet: the interactions treat all directions alike, yet the magnetisation picks one (<a href=\"#c-beyond\" data-open=\"beyond\">12 · Symmetry breaking and group theory today</a>).","ch":"beyond"}],"books":[{"title":"Chemical Applications of Group Theory","authors":"F. Albert Cotton (Wiley, 3rd ed. 1990)","note":"The classic textbook of group theory for chemists: point groups, character tables, orbitals, vibrations and ligand fields in turn, with all the common character tables in the appendix. Read alongside chapters 2 to 7."},{"title":"Symmetry and Spectroscopy: An Introduction to Vibrational and Electronic Spectroscopy","authors":"Daniel C. Harris, Michael D. Bertolucci (Oxford, 1978; Dover reprint 1989)","note":"Chapter 1 has the point-group flow chart (the demo in chapter 2 is redrawn following its order of questions); later chapters connect group theory to IR, Raman and UV spectra very intuitively."},{"title":"Molecular Symmetry and Group Theory","authors":"Robert L. Carter (Wiley, 1998)","note":"Thinner than Cotton with more worked examples; good for self-study of chapters 1 to 6."},{"title":"Group Theory and Its Application to the Quantum Mechanics of Atomic Spectra","authors":"Eugene P. Wigner (Academic Press, 1959; German original 1931)","note":"The book that brought group theory into quantum mechanics: the rotation group, angular momentum, the Wigner–Eckart theorem, time reversal. The source of chapters 9 and 10."},{"title":"Group Theory: Application to the Physics of Condensed Matter","authors":"Mildred S. Dresselhaus, Gene Dresselhaus, Ado Jorio (Springer, 2008)","note":"A book grown out of MIT lecture notes: from point groups to space groups, bands, phonons and Raman. Further reading for chapters 8 and 11."},{"title":"Group Theory in a Nutshell for Physicists","authors":"A. Zee (Princeton, 2016)","note":"Group theory in a physicist’s voice: Lie groups, SU(2), SU(3), the Lorentz group and gauge theory. A good companion to chapters 9 and 12."},{"title":"Symmetry","authors":"Hermann Weyl (Princeton, 1952)","note":"A short book that goes from ornaments and crystals to the symmetries of physical law; good at the very beginning or the very end."}],"open":[{"q":"What mathematics properly describes the “symmetry” of a quasicrystal? Projection from a higher-dimensional periodic lattice explains most of it, but whether quasicrystals are stabilised by energy or entropy, and how they grow, are still debated.","ch":"crystals"},{"q":"Symmetry indicators detect only part of band topology; how to find “fragile” topology and topological phases invisible to indicators systematically has no complete answer yet.","ch":"bands"},{"q":"The “moonshine” link between the Monster group and modular functions was proved by Borcherds, but why it exists, and whether deeper physics lies behind it (string theory offers one candidate explanation), remain open.","ch":"beyond"},{"q":"Equivariant neural networks hard-wire rotational symmetry into their structure and are very data-efficient; but whether symmetry should be hard-wired or learned from data (augmentation) has become contested again in the era of large models.","ch":"beyond"}],"notes":{"map":"Each card is a chapter; chapter numbers give the suggested reading order (chapters 1 to 4 are the foundation for everything after). The label gives the status of the bulk of the chapter; key points inside carry their own labels. Chapters you have read are marked (on this device only).","history":"Years are publication years.","path":"Chemistry readers: go 1 → 2 → 3 → 4, then pick 5 (orbitals), 6 (vibrational spectra) and 7 (ligand fields). Physics readers: after 1 → 3 → 4, read 9 (Lie groups), 10 (angular momentum), 11 (bands) and 12. After chapter 6, see real IR and Raman spectra in the <a href=\"../spectra/\">Spectra hall</a>; the space groups of chapter 8 are put to work in <a href=\"../optics/#c-xray\">Optics · X-ray crystal diffraction</a> and <a href=\"../chemistry/structural/\">Structural chemistry</a>; the Clebsch–Gordan coefficients of chapter 10 are exactly the tensor products of the equivariant networks in <a href=\"../nequip/#c-tensor\">NequIP</a>; the bands of chapter 11 connect to <a href=\"../quasiparticles/\">Quasiparticles</a>.","library":"54 references. Each was looked up by DOI in OpenAlex (data from Crossref) for title, authors, year and volume, and those without a DOI on arXiv (November 2026; node tools/group-refs.mjs rechecks them)."}},"refs":[{"id":"cayley1854","authors":"A. Cayley","year":1854,"title":"On the theory of groups, as depending on the symbolic equation θⁿ = 1","journal":"Philosophical Magazine","vol":"7","pages":"40–47","doi":"10.1080/14786445408647421","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["what"]},{"id":"killing1888","authors":"W. Killing","year":1888,"title":"Die Zusammensetzung der stetigen endlichen Transformations-gruppen","journal":"Mathematische Annalen","vol":"31","pages":"252–290","doi":"10.1007/BF01211904","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["lie"]},{"id":"noether1918","authors":"E. Noether","year":1918,"title":"Invariant variation problems","journal":"Transport Theory and Statistical Physics","vol":"1","pages":"186–207","doi":"10.1080/00411457108231446","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"Originally published in 1918 in the Nachrichten of the Göttingen Royal Society; this is the 1971 English translation by M. A. Tavel.","used":["what","lie"]},{"id":"polya1924","authors":"G. Pólya","year":1924,"title":"Über die Analogie der Kristallsymmetrie in der Ebene","journal":"Zeitschrift für Kristallographie","vol":"60","pages":"278–282","doi":"10.1524/zkri.1924.60.1.278","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["crystals"]},{"id":"laporte1925","authors":"O. Laporte, W. F. Meggers","year":1925,"title":"Some rules of spectral structure","journal":"Journal of the Optical Society of America","vol":"11","pages":"459–463","doi":"10.1364/JOSA.11.000459","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["reps","orbitals"]},{"id":"bethe1929","authors":"H. Bethe","year":1929,"title":"Termaufspaltung in Kristallen","journal":"Annalen der Physik","vol":"395","pages":"133–208","doi":"10.1002/andp.19293950202","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["reps","ligand"]},{"id":"bloch1929","authors":"F. Bloch","year":1929,"title":"Über die Quantenmechanik der Elektronen in Kristallgittern","journal":"Zeitschrift für Physik","vol":"52","pages":"555–600","doi":"10.1007/BF01339455","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["bands"]},{"id":"eckart1930","authors":"C. Eckart","year":1930,"title":"The Application of Group theory to the Quantum Dynamics of Monatomic Systems","journal":"Reviews of Modern Physics","vol":"2","pages":"305–380","doi":"10.1103/RevModPhys.2.305","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["angular"]},{"id":"huckel1931","authors":"E. Hückel","year":1931,"title":"Quantentheoretische Beiträge zum Benzolproblem. I. Die Elektronenkonfiguration des Benzols und verwandter Verbindungen","journal":"Zeitschrift für Physik","vol":"70","pages":"204–286","doi":"10.1007/BF01339530","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["orbitals"]},{"id":"vanvleck1932","authors":"J. H. Van Vleck","year":1932,"title":"Theory of the Variations in Paramagnetic Anisotropy Among Different Salts of the Iron Group","journal":"Physical Review","vol":"41","pages":"208–215","doi":"10.1103/PhysRev.41.208","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["ligand"]},{"id":"wilson1934","authors":"E. B. Wilson","year":1934,"title":"The Normal Modes and Frequencies of Vibration of the Regular Plane Hexagon Model of the Benzene Molecule","journal":"Physical Review","vol":"45","pages":"706–714","doi":"10.1103/PhysRev.45.706","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["vibrations"]},{"id":"bsw1936","authors":"L. P. Bouckaert, R. Smoluchowski, E. Wigner","year":1936,"title":"Theory of Brillouin Zones and Symmetry Properties of Wave Functions in Crystals","journal":"Physical Review","vol":"50","pages":"58–67","doi":"10.1103/PhysRev.50.58","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["bands"]},{"id":"herring1937","authors":"C. Herring","year":1937,"title":"Effect of Time-Reversal Symmetry on Energy Bands of Crystals","journal":"Physical Review","vol":"52","pages":"361–365","doi":"10.1103/PhysRev.52.361","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["bands"]},{"id":"jahnteller1937","authors":"H. A. Jahn, E. Teller","year":1937,"title":"Stability of polyatomic molecules in degenerate electronic states. I. Orbital degeneracy","journal":"Proceedings of the Royal Society A","vol":"161","pages":"220–235","doi":"10.1098/rspa.1937.0142","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["ligand"]},{"id":"wigner1939","authors":"E. Wigner","year":1939,"title":"On Unitary Representations of the Inhomogeneous Lorentz Group","journal":"Annals of Mathematics","vol":"40","pages":"149–204","doi":"10.2307/1968551","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["beyond"]},{"id":"wilson1939","authors":"E. B. Wilson","year":1939,"title":"A Method of Obtaining the Expanded Secular Equation for the Vibration Frequencies of a Molecule","journal":"Journal of Chemical Physics","vol":"7","pages":"1047–1052","doi":"10.1063/1.1750363","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["vibrations"]},{"id":"racah1942","authors":"G. Racah","year":1942,"title":"Theory of Complex Spectra. II","journal":"Physical Review","vol":"62","pages":"438–462","doi":"10.1103/PhysRev.62.438","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["angular"]},{"id":"frost1953","authors":"A. A. Frost, B. Musulin","year":1953,"title":"A Mnemonic Device for Molecular Orbital Energies","journal":"Journal of Chemical Physics","vol":"21","pages":"572–573","doi":"10.1063/1.1698970","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["orbitals"]},{"id":"tanabe1954","authors":"Y. Tanabe, S. Sugano","year":1954,"title":"On the Absorption Spectra of Complex Ions. I","journal":"Journal of the Physical Society of Japan","vol":"9","pages":"753–766","doi":"10.1143/JPSJ.9.753","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["ligand"]},{"id":"yangmills1954","authors":"C. N. Yang, R. L. Mills","year":1954,"title":"Conservation of Isotopic Spin and Isotopic Gauge Invariance","journal":"Physical Review","vol":"96","pages":"191–195","doi":"10.1103/PhysRev.96.191","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["lie"]},{"id":"mulliken1955","authors":"R. S. Mulliken","year":1955,"title":"Report on Notation for the Spectra of Polyatomic Molecules","journal":"Journal of Chemical Physics","vol":"23","pages":"1997–2011","doi":"10.1063/1.1740655","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["pointgroups","reps"]},{"id":"leeyang1956","authors":"T. D. Lee, C. N. Yang","year":1956,"title":"Question of Parity Conservation in Weak Interactions","journal":"Physical Review","vol":"104","pages":"254–258","doi":"10.1103/PhysRev.104.254","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["beyond"]},{"id":"wu1957","authors":"C. S. Wu, E. Ambler, R. W. Hayward, D. D. Hoppes, R. P. Hudson","year":1957,"title":"Experimental Test of Parity Conservation in Beta Decay","journal":"Physical Review","vol":"105","pages":"1413–1415","doi":"10.1103/PhysRev.105.1413","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["beyond"]},{"id":"goldstone1961","authors":"J. Goldstone","year":1961,"title":"Field theories with “superconductor” solutions","journal":"Il Nuovo Cimento","vol":"19","pages":"154–164","doi":"10.1007/BF02812722","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["beyond"]},{"id":"neeman1961","authors":"Y. Ne’eman","year":1961,"title":"Derivation of strong interactions from a gauge invariance","journal":"Nuclear Physics","vol":"26","pages":"222–229","doi":"10.1016/0029-5582(61)90134-1","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["lie"]},{"id":"gellmann1962","authors":"M. Gell-Mann","year":1962,"title":"Symmetries of Baryons and Mesons","journal":"Physical Review","vol":"125","pages":"1067–1084","doi":"10.1103/PhysRev.125.1067","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["lie"]},{"id":"gsw1962","authors":"J. Goldstone, A. Salam, S. Weinberg","year":1962,"title":"Broken Symmetries","journal":"Physical Review","vol":"127","pages":"965–970","doi":"10.1103/PhysRev.127.965","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["beyond"]},{"id":"englert1964","authors":"F. Englert, R. Brout","year":1964,"title":"Broken Symmetry and the Mass of Gauge Vector Mesons","journal":"Physical Review Letters","vol":"13","pages":"321–323","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.13.321","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["beyond"]},{"id":"gellmann1964","authors":"M. Gell-Mann","year":1964,"title":"A schematic model of baryons and mesons","journal":"Physics Letters","vol":"8","pages":"214–215","doi":"10.1016/S0031-9163(64)92001-3","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["lie"]},{"id":"higgs1964","authors":"P. W. Higgs","year":1964,"title":"Broken Symmetries and the Masses of Gauge Bosons","journal":"Physical Review Letters","vol":"13","pages":"508–509","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.13.508","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["beyond"]},{"id":"schaffer1965","authors":"C. E. Schäffer, C. K. Jørgensen","year":1965,"title":"The angular overlap model, an attempt to revive the ligand field approaches","journal":"Molecular Physics","vol":"9","pages":"401–412","doi":"10.1080/00268976500100551","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["ligand"]},{"id":"woodward1965","authors":"R. B. Woodward, R. Hoffmann","year":1965,"title":"Stereochemistry of Electrocyclic Reactions","journal":"Journal of the American Chemical Society","vol":"87","pages":"395–397","doi":"10.1021/ja01080a054","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["orbitals"]},{"id":"anderson1972","authors":"P. W. Anderson","year":1972,"title":"More is different","journal":"Science","vol":"177","pages":"393–396","doi":"10.1126/science.177.4047.393","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["beyond"]},{"id":"shimanouchi1972","authors":"T. Shimanouchi","year":1972,"title":"Tables of molecular vibrational frequencies, consolidated volume I","journal":"NSRDS-NBS 39 (National Bureau of Standards)","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"10.6028/NBS.NSRDS.39","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["vibrations"]},{"id":"rauch1975","authors":"H. Rauch, A. Zeilinger, G. Badurek et al.","year":1975,"title":"Verification of coherent spinor rotation of fermions","journal":"Physics Letters A","vol":"54","pages":"425–427","doi":"10.1016/0375-9601(75)90798-7","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["lie"]},{"id":"werner1975","authors":"S. A. Werner, R. Colella, A. W. Overhauser, C. F. Eagen","year":1975,"title":"Observation of the Phase Shift of a Neutron Due to Precession in a Magnetic Field","journal":"Physical Review Letters","vol":"35","pages":"1053–1055","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.35.1053","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["lie"]},{"id":"griess1982","authors":"R. L. Griess","year":1982,"title":"The friendly giant","journal":"Inventiones mathematicae","vol":"69","pages":"1–102","doi":"10.1007/BF01389186","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["what","beyond"]},{"id":"shechtman1984","authors":"D. Shechtman, I. Blech, D. Gratias, J. W. Cahn","year":1984,"title":"Metallic Phase with Long-Range Orientational Order and No Translational Symmetry","journal":"Physical Review Letters","vol":"53","pages":"1951–1953","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.53.1951","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["crystals"]},{"id":"borcherds1992","authors":"R. E. Borcherds","year":1992,"title":"Monstrous moonshine and monstrous Lie superalgebras","journal":"Inventiones mathematicae","vol":"109","pages":"405–444","doi":"10.1007/BF01232032","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["beyond"]},{"id":"gross1996","authors":"D. J. Gross","year":1996,"title":"The role of symmetry in fundamental physics","journal":"PNAS","vol":"93","pages":"14256–14259","doi":"10.1073/pnas.93.25.14256","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["what","beyond"]},{"id":"kanemele2005","authors":"C. L. Kane, E. J. Mele","year":2005,"title":"Quantum Spin Hall Effect in Graphene","journal":"Physical Review Letters","vol":"95","pages":"226801","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.95.226801","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["bands"]},{"id":"aroyo2006","authors":"M. I. Aroyo, J. M. Perez-Mato, C. Capillas et al.","year":2006,"title":"Bilbao Crystallographic Server: I. Databases and crystallographic computing programs","journal":"Zeitschrift für Kristallographie","vol":"221","pages":"15–27","doi":"10.1524/zkri.2006.221.1.15","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["crystals"]},{"id":"fu2011","authors":"L. Fu","year":2011,"title":"Topological Crystalline Insulators","journal":"Physical Review Letters","vol":"106","pages":"106802","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.106.106802","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["bands"]},{"id":"cohen2016","authors":"T. S. Cohen, M. Welling","year":2016,"title":"Group Equivariant Convolutional Networks","journal":"arXiv preprint","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"","arxiv":"1602.07576","url":"","note":"","used":["beyond"]},{"id":"bradlyn2017","authors":"B. Bradlyn, L. Elcoro, J. Cano et al.","year":2017,"title":"Topological quantum chemistry","journal":"Nature","vol":"547","pages":"298–305","doi":"10.1038/nature23268","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["bands"]},{"id":"po2017","authors":"H. C. Po, A. Vishwanath, H. Watanabe","year":2017,"title":"Symmetry-based indicators of band topology in the 230 space groups","journal":"Nature Communications","vol":"8","pages":"50","doi":"10.1038/s41467-017-00133-2","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["bands"]},{"id":"thomas2018","authors":"N. Thomas, T. Smidt, S. Kearnes et al.","year":2018,"title":"Tensor field networks: Rotation- and translation-equivariant neural networks for 3D point clouds","journal":"arXiv preprint","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"","arxiv":"1802.08219","url":"","note":"","used":["beyond"]},{"id":"weiler2018","authors":"M. Weiler, M. Geiger, M. Welling et al.","year":2018,"title":"3D Steerable CNNs: Learning Rotationally Equivariant Features in Volumetric Data","journal":"arXiv preprint","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"","arxiv":"1807.02547","url":"","note":"","used":["beyond"]},{"id":"tang2019","authors":"F. Tang, H. C. Po, A. Vishwanath, X. Wan","year":2019,"title":"Comprehensive search for topological materials using symmetry indicators","journal":"Nature","vol":"566","pages":"486–489","doi":"10.1038/s41586-019-0937-5","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["bands"]},{"id":"vergniory2019","authors":"M. G. Vergniory, L. Elcoro, C. Felser et al.","year":2019,"title":"A complete catalogue of high-quality topological materials","journal":"Nature","vol":"566","pages":"480–485","doi":"10.1038/s41586-019-0954-4","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["bands"]},{"id":"zhang2019","authors":"T. Zhang, Y. Jiang, Z. Song et al.","year":2019,"title":"Catalogue of topological electronic materials","journal":"Nature","vol":"566","pages":"475–479","doi":"10.1038/s41586-019-0944-6","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["bands"]},{"id":"batzner2022","authors":"S. Batzner, A. Musaelian, L. Sun et al.","year":2022,"title":"E(3)-equivariant graph neural networks for data-efficient and accurate interatomic potentials","journal":"Nature Communications","vol":"13","pages":"2453","doi":"10.1038/s41467-022-29939-5","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["beyond"]},{"id":"geiger2022","authors":"M. Geiger, T. Smidt","year":2022,"title":"e3nn: Euclidean Neural Networks","journal":"arXiv preprint","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"","arxiv":"2207.09453","url":"","note":"","used":["beyond"]},{"id":"togo2024","authors":"A. Togo, K. Shinohara, I. Tanaka","year":2024,"title":"Spglib: a software library for crystal symmetry search","journal":"Science and Technology of Advanced Materials Methods","vol":"4","pages":"2384822","doi":"10.1080/27660400.2024.2384822","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["crystals"]}],"chapters":{"what":{"id":"what","body":[{"p":"Take an equilateral triangle. Rotate it about its centre by 120° or 240°, or flip it over one of its three medians, and it looks exactly as before; doing nothing of course leaves it unchanged too. These six operations are the <b>symmetry operations</b> of the triangle. Doing two in a row gives another of the six; every operation has one that undoes it; and for three operations in a fixed order it does not matter how you bracket them. Mathematicians call anything with these properties a <b>group</b>."},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>⇒</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>e</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The four axioms: closure, associativity, identity, inverses."},{"p":"Notice that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is not required. For the triangle, rotating by 120° then flipping gives a different flip from flipping then rotating. Groups where order never matters are <b>abelian</b>, such as the rotations-only group <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>; the others are non-abelian, such as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, the full set of six operations of the triangle. Molecular point groups, crystal space groups and the rotation group of quantum mechanics are mostly non-abelian, which is exactly what makes them interesting: only non-abelian groups produce degeneracies (<a href=\"#c-reps\" data-open=\"reps\">03 · Representations and character tables</a>)."},{"p":"In 1854 Cayley first wrote a group as pure symbols: a set of symbols plus a table saying what any two multiply to, whatever the symbols stand for, rotations, permutations or numbers <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-cayley1854\" data-ref=\"cayley1854\">Cayley 1854</a>]</span>. This <b>multiplication table</b> (Cayley table) contains everything about the group. It has a pretty property: every row and every column uses each element exactly once, like a sudoku. The reason is simple: if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi><mi>b</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, multiply both sides on the left by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> to get <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></semantics></math>."},{"demo":"cayley"},{"h":"Order, subgroups and Lagrange’s theorem"},{"p":"The number of elements in a group is its <b>order</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>G</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. Multiply an element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></semantics></math> by itself enough times and you return to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi></mrow></semantics></math>; the smallest number of times is the element’s order. A subset that itself satisfies the four axioms is a <b>subgroup</b>. Take a subgroup <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and multiply each of its elements on the left by the same <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></semantics></math>: the set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mi>H</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is a <b>left coset</b>. Distinct cosets do not overlap and all have the same size, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, so they cut the whole group into equal blocks:"},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>G</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mo>⟹</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mtext> </mtext><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">∣</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>G</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":""},{"p":"This is <b>Lagrange’s theorem</b>. It tells you at once that a group of order 6 has no subgroup of order 4, that every element’s order divides the group’s order, and that a group of prime order must be cyclic. In the demo, choose a subgroup and the table is coloured by cosets: the blocks are all the same size."},{"h":"Conjugacy classes: operations “of the same kind”"},{"p":"If there is a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></semantics></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow></semantics></math> are <b>conjugate</b>. Intuitively, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is “<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></semantics></math> seen from another angle”. The triangle’s three flips are all conjugate (rotate the coordinate frame and one flip becomes another), the two rotations <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></semantics></math> are conjugate, and the identity is a class by itself. So the six elements of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> fall into <b>conjugacy classes</b> of sizes 1 + 2 + 3. Classes matter enormously later: characters of a representation are constant on each class, and the number of irreducible representations equals the number of classes (<a href=\"#c-reps\" data-open=\"reps\">03 · Representations and character tables</a>). If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mi>H</mi><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></semantics></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, the subgroup <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is <b>normal</b>, and then the cosets themselves form a group (the quotient group)."},{"h":"Why symmetry is so useful"},{"p":"In quantum mechanics a symmetry operation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow></semantics></math> that leaves the Hamiltonian unchanged (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>H</mi><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></semantics></math>) commutes with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. So an eigenstate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></semantics></math> can only be sent by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow></semantics></math> to another eigenstate of the same energy: the eigenstates of one level are “stirred” among themselves by the group, forming a <b>representation</b> of it. Without solving any equation you know how degenerate a level can be, what its wavefunctions look like and which matrix elements must vanish. Continuous symmetries also give conserved quantities: time translation gives energy conservation, space translation momentum, rotation angular momentum (Noether’s theorem <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-noether1918\" data-ref=\"noether1918\">Noether 1918</a>]</span>). Physicists say “symmetry dictates interaction” <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-gross1996\" data-ref=\"gross1996\">Gross 1996</a>]</span>; chemists say “one look at the point group tells you how many bands the spectrum has”. It is the same idea."},{"note":"<b>The classification of finite groups is complete.</b> Every finite group is built from “simple” groups like bricks; twentieth-century mathematicians proved, in tens of thousands of pages, that the finite simple groups are a few infinite families plus 26 “sporadic” exceptions. The largest exception, the Monster, has order about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>53</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> and was constructed by Griess in 1982 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-griess1982\" data-ref=\"griess1982\">Griess 1982</a>]</span>.","kind":"info"}],"points":[{"h":"Four axioms","p":"Closure, associativity, identity, inverses; commutativity is not required.","st":"proved"},{"h":"The multiplication table is a Latin square","p":"Each row and column uses every element exactly once, because inverses can be cancelled on either side.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Lagrange’s theorem","p":"The order of a subgroup divides the order of the group; so does the order of every element.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Conjugacy classes","p":"gag⁻¹ is “a seen from another angle”; the group splits into disjoint classes, and characters later take one value per class.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Symmetry and conservation laws","p":"Operations commuting with H transform states of one level among themselves; continuous symmetries give conserved quantities (Noether).","st":"proved"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>G</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Lagrange’s theorem: the number of cosets (the index) times the order of the subgroup."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>:</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>G</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mtext>classes</mtext></munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Conjugacy classes and the class equation."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext>  </mtext><mo>⇒</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"A symmetry operation sends an eigenstate to an eigenstate of the same energy."}],"example":{"q":"The symmetry group <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> of a square has 8 elements: rotations by 0°, 90°, 180°, 270° and four flips (two through midpoints of opposite edges, two through diagonals). What are its conjugacy classes? Does it have a subgroup of order 3?","steps":["The identity and the 180° rotation commute with everything, so each is a class by itself. The 90° and 270° rotations are conjugate (flip the square over and clockwise becomes anticlockwise).","The two “midline” flips are conjugate (a 90° turn swaps them), as are the two “diagonal” flips; but a midline flip is not conjugate to a diagonal flip, since no symmetry sends edge midpoints to corners.","So the class sizes are 1 + 1 + 2 + 2 + 2 = 8. Since 3 does not divide 8, Lagrange’s theorem rules out a subgroup of order 3."],"a":"Five classes of sizes 1, 1, 2, 2, 2; no subgroup of order 3. Check it in the demo: pick <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and colour by conjugacy class."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-cayley1854\" data-ref=\"cayley1854\">Cayley 1854</a> · <a href=\"#ref-noether1918\" data-ref=\"noether1918\">Noether 1918</a> · <a href=\"#ref-griess1982\" data-ref=\"griess1982\">Griess 1982</a> · <a href=\"#ref-gross1996\" data-ref=\"gross1996\">Gross 1996</a>"},"pointgroups":{"id":"pointgroups","body":[{"p":"A symmetry operation of a molecule makes it <b>indistinguishable</b> from how it was: afterwards every atom sits where an atom of the same element used to be. Every symmetry operation of a finite molecule leaves the centre of mass fixed, so the group they form is called a <b>point group</b>. An operation is an action; a symmetry element is the geometric object it is performed about (an axis, a plane, a point)."},{"table":{"head":["Element","Operation","Example"],"rows":[["Identity E","Do nothing","Every molecule"],["n-fold axis Cₙ","Rotate by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></semantics></math> about the axis","C₂ of H₂O, C₃ of NH₃, C₆ of benzene"],["Mirror plane σ","Reflect through the plane","σh ⊥ principal axis, σv contains it, σd contains it and bisects two C₂ axes"],["Centre of symmetry i","Every point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mo>→</mo><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi></mrow></semantics></math>","SF₆, trans-N₂F₂, CO₂"],["Improper axis Sₙ","Rotate by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></semantics></math> then reflect through the perpendicular plane","S₄ of CH₄ (which has neither C₄ nor σh)"]],"cap":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, so the five really come in two kinds: proper and improper rotations."}},{"p":"Point groups are named in <b>Schoenflies notation</b>: the letter says which kinds of element are present and the subscript gives the order of the principal axis. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> has a single n-fold axis; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> adds n mirrors containing the axis; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> adds one mirror perpendicular to it; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> plus n perpendicular <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> axes; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> add mirrors to that; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> has only an improper axis. A few “special” high-symmetry groups complete the list: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> for linear molecules, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> for the tetrahedron, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> for the octahedron and cube, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> for the icosahedron (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>12</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></semantics></math>, C₆₀). Molecular spectroscopy, quantum-chemistry programs and crystallographic databases all use these names (crystallography also has Hermann–Mauguin symbols, <a href=\"#c-crystals\" data-open=\"crystals\">08 · Crystals: translations, space groups and why there are no five-fold axes</a>)."},{"h":"The flow chart"},{"p":"You do not need to find every operation to identify a point group: answer a few questions in order and each step halves the candidates. The order here follows the textbook by Harris and Bertolucci (see “Reading”):"},{"steps":["<b>A special group?</b> Linear: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> with a centre of symmetry (CO₂, N₂), otherwise <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> (HCN, CO). More than one axis with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></semantics></math>: the tetrahedral, octahedral or icosahedral families.","<b>Any <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>)?</b> If not: a mirror gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, a centre of symmetry <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, neither <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>.","If so, pick the highest as the <b>principal axis</b> (call it z). If there is no mirror at all, ask: is there an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> along the principal axis and no ⊥<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>? Then it is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>.","<b>n <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> axes perpendicular to the principal axis?</b> Yes: the D family. Then ask for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>), then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>); with neither it is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>.","No: the C family. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, n <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, neither gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>."]},{"p":"The demo below redraws the chart so you can click it. Pick a molecule and the program first finds all its symmetry elements from the atomic coordinates (it is not told the answer), then walks the chart and lights the path in gold; click a lit box and the 3D view draws the element used at that step; drag “Perform the operation” to watch where the operation sends the molecule."},{"demo":"flow"},{"h":"Two examples"},{"p":"<b>trans-1-Bromo-2-chloroethylene, BrHC=CHCl.</b> Not linear, not high-symmetry. Any <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>? If both ends were the same (trans-1,2-dichloroethylene) there would be a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> perpendicular to the molecular plane through the midpoint of C=C; but here one end has Br and the other Cl, and a half-turn would put Br where Cl was, so there is <b>no</b> rotation axis at all. Any mirror? The molecule is planar, and the molecular plane is a mirror (reflection sends every atom to itself). Answer: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, with just two operations <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>E</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow></semantics></math>."},{"p":"<b>Dichloromethane, CH₂Cl₂.</b> Tetrahedral in shape, but the four substituents are two different pairs, so not <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>. There is one <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, bisecting the H–C–H and Cl–C–Cl angles. Only this one, with no perpendicular <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>: the C family. No <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> (a plane perpendicular to the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> would reflect H onto empty space). Two mirrors contain the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, one holding both H and one holding both Cl. Answer: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, with four operations <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>E</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>v</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow></semantics></math>, the same as water."},{"note":"<b>Two common traps.</b> One is ethane: staggered is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> (mirrors between pairs of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> axes), eclipsed is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> (it has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>); both are in the demo. The other is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>: methane has three <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> axes but no <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, and a molecule such as 1,3,5,7-tetramethylcyclooctatetraene keeps a single <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and nothing else, belonging to the point group <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> (the “S₄ model” in the demo).","kind":"warn"},{"h":"Your own molecule"},{"p":"The flow chart is only a set of questions; the answers have to come from coordinates. The tool below takes coordinates in XYZ format: the output of a geometry optimisation, a molecule cut out of a crystal structure, or a few lines typed by hand. Real coordinates are never exactly symmetric: an optimisation converges to about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> Å, and a crystal structure carries thermal motion and measurement error, so before deciding anything we must say how different still counts as the same. That is the <b>tolerance</b>. The program finds every symmetry element within the tolerance, generates the whole group from them, and replaces each atom by the average of its images under all the operations, giving exactly symmetric coordinates (quantum-chemistry programs do the same before a calculation starts; Gaussian's Symmetry=Loose keyword loosens exactly this tolerance). Make the tolerance smaller and weak symmetry is lost; make it larger and a nearly symmetric molecule is lifted into a higher group, so a slightly twisted ethane is taken for the staggered one."},{"demo":"xyz"},{"p":"Why do point groups matter? Because molecules in the same point group share one <b>character table</b> (<a href=\"#c-reps\" data-open=\"reps\">03 · Representations and character tables</a>): water and dichloromethane are both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, and their orbitals, vibrations and selection rules have exactly the same form. Spectroscopists label levels and vibrations with the notation Mulliken standardised <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-mulliken1955\" data-ref=\"mulliken1955\">Mulliken 1955</a>]</span>."}],"points":[{"h":"Five kinds of element","p":"E, Cₙ, σ, i, Sₙ; with S₁ = σ and S₂ = i.","st":"proved"},{"h":"A point group fixes at least one point","p":"All symmetry operations of a finite molecule fix the centre of mass.","st":"proved"},{"h":"The flow chart","p":"Special groups → Cₙ → ⊥C₂ → σh → σv (σd); with no rotation axis, look for σ and i.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Same point group, same table","p":"H₂O and CH₂Cl₂ are both C2v, and their selection rules have exactly the same form.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Real molecules are only nearly symmetric","p":"Vibrations and isotopes break symmetry momentarily; the point group describes the equilibrium geometry.","st":"approx"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>:</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Proper and improper rotations."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>n</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>24</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>48</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>120</mn></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Orders of some point groups."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">n</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mtext> </mtext><mn mathvariant=\"double-struck\">1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">n</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>×</mo></msub><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">n</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msup><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">n</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">T</mi></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The matrix for a rotation by θ about a unit axis n (Rodrigues’ formula); every operation in the demo is built from it."}],"example":{"q":"Use the flow chart to find the point groups of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> (square planar) and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> (trigonal pyramidal).","steps":["XeF₄: not special. The highest axis is the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> perpendicular to the plane. Four <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> axes lie in the plane (two along Xe–F, two between): the D family. The molecular plane is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>. Answer <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, 16 operations.","NH₃: not special. The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> runs through N and the centre of the three H. No perpendicular <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> (N sits on one side): the C family. No <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> (N is not in the plane of the three H). Three mirrors each contain the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and one N–H bond. Answer <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, 6 operations."],"a":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>. Both are in the demo, so you can check whether the program takes the same path as you."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-mulliken1955\" data-ref=\"mulliken1955\">Mulliken 1955</a>"},"reps":{"id":"reps","body":[{"p":"A <b>representation</b> of a group assigns an invertible matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> to each element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></semantics></math> so that matrix multiplication copies the group’s: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>. The most direct example: point-group operations acting on the coordinates <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> give a set of 3 × 3 matrices. But that is not the only one. The two H 1s orbitals of water, the nine displacements of its three atoms, a set of d orbitals: each is mixed among itself by the symmetry operations and gives a representation. <b>One group has endlessly many representations</b>, which is exactly why they are useful: any “set of things that symmetry operations mix among themselves” is a basis for one."},{"p":"Change basis (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>S</mi><mi>D</mi><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>) and every matrix changes, but the representation is “essentially” the same: an <b>equivalent representation</b>. If some basis makes all the matrices block-diagonal at once, the representation is <b>reducible</b> and splits into a direct sum of smaller ones; pieces that cannot be split further are <b>irreducible representations</b> (irreps). In <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></semantics></math> alone is a one-dimensional representation (every operation leaves <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></semantics></math> unchanged), while <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> is turned into mixtures of itself by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and cannot be separated: a two-dimensional irrep, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow></semantics></math>."},{"h":"Characters: one number instead of a matrix"},{"p":"Matrices are many and carry too much information. The <b>character</b> of a representation is the trace of each matrix, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>. Traces do not change under a change of basis, so equivalent representations have the same characters, and so do elements of the same class (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mi>D</mi><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mi>D</mi></mrow></semantics></math>). A representation is therefore recorded with one number per class. Even better, <b>a representation of a finite group is completely determined by its characters</b>, thanks to this theorem:"},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>h</mi></mfrac><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></munder><msup><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>∗</mo></msup><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Orthogonality of characters: the character “vectors” of different irreps are orthogonal (h is the order of the group)."},{"p":"It follows from Schur’s lemma and the “great orthogonality theorem”. Several structural rules for character tables follow, enough to check a table or complete one from scratch:"},{"list":["The number of irreps <b>equals</b> the number of conjugacy classes, so the table is square.","The squared dimensions of the irreps add up to the group order: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></semantics></math>; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>48</mn></mrow></semantics></math>.","Rows are orthogonal when weighted by class sizes, and so are columns. One row is always all 1s: the totally symmetric representation.","The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow></semantics></math> column gives the dimensions (the trace of an identity matrix)."]},{"demo":"chartable"},{"h":"How to read a character table"},{"p":"The top-left corner names the point group, the first row lists the classes (“<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>” means the class has two elements), and the left column gives the irreps in <b>Mulliken notation</b> <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-mulliken1955\" data-ref=\"mulliken1955\">Mulliken 1955</a>]</span>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></semantics></math> are one-dimensional (symmetric or antisymmetric under the principal rotation), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow></semantics></math> two-dimensional, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></semantics></math> three-dimensional; subscripts g and u mean symmetric (gerade) or antisymmetric (ungerade) under inversion; primes ′ and ″ mean symmetric or antisymmetric under <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>; subscripts 1 and 2 distinguish symmetry under a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>. The two right-hand columns list functions that transform as each irrep: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></semantics></math> (like the electric dipole, deciding IR activity and electric-dipole transitions), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>z</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> (rotations) and quadratic functions <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>y</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>…</mo></mrow></semantics></math> (like the polarisability, deciding Raman activity; also the shapes of the d orbitals). Bethe was the first to apply such tables to the splitting of atomic levels in crystals, in 1929 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bethe1929\" data-ref=\"bethe1929\">Bethe 1929</a>]</span>."},{"h":"Direct products and integrals that must vanish"},{"p":"The character of a direct (tensor) product of two representations is the product of their characters. This gives the rule chemists use most: an integral <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mtext> </mtext><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>O</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> can be non-zero only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>⊗</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mi>O</mi></msub><mo>⊗</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> contains the totally symmetric representation. The reason: the integral is over all space, so its value must be unchanged by every symmetry operation; an integrand with no totally symmetric part cancels between its positive and negative halves. In one stroke this decides which atomic orbitals can combine into molecular orbitals (<a href=\"#c-orbitals\" data-open=\"orbitals\">05 · Molecular orbitals: symmetry fixes the shape of the answer first</a>), which vibrations show in IR or Raman (<a href=\"#c-vibrations\" data-open=\"vibrations\">06 · Vibrational spectra: counting atoms that stay put</a>) and which electronic transitions are allowed (the Laporte rule <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-laporte1925\" data-ref=\"laporte1925\">Laporte &amp; Meggers 1925</a>]</span>). Click two rows in the demo and the readout says whether their product contains the totally symmetric irrep."}],"points":[{"h":"Representations","p":"D(g)D(h) = D(gh); any set of things mixed among themselves by symmetry operations is a basis for one.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Characters determine representations","p":"Two representations of a finite group are equivalent if and only if they have the same characters.","st":"proved"},{"h":"The table is square","p":"Number of irreps = number of classes; Σ dᵢ² = h; rows and columns are orthogonal.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Degeneracy needs non-commutativity","p":"Every irrep of an abelian group is one-dimensional, so there is no symmetry-protected degeneracy.","st":"proved"},{"h":"The vanishing-integral rule","p":"⟨a|Ô|b⟩ can be non-zero only if Γa ⊗ ΓO ⊗ Γb contains the totally symmetric irrep.","st":"proved"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>g</mi></munder><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>∗</mo></msup><mtext> </mtext><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><msup><mi>n</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The great orthogonality theorem (for unitary irreps)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">#</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mtext>irreps</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">#</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mtext>classes</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Two structural rules for character tables."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>⊗</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Characters of a direct product."}],"example":{"q":"The classes of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> are known. Find the characters of the third irrep using only the structural rules.","steps":["Three classes, so three irreps. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></semantics></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></semantics></math>.","Write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>. Orthogonal to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math>; to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math>.","Subtracting gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>. Its inner product with itself is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>6</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>: it is indeed irreducible."],"a":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>. This is the representation carried by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> turns by 120° with trace <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>120</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">°</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>; a mirror keeps one direction and flips the other, trace 0."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-laporte1925\" data-ref=\"laporte1925\">Laporte &amp; Meggers 1925</a> · <a href=\"#ref-bethe1929\" data-ref=\"bethe1929\">Bethe 1929</a> · <a href=\"#ref-mulliken1955\" data-ref=\"mulliken1955\">Mulliken 1955</a>"},"reduce":{"id":"reduce","body":[{"p":"The orthogonality relations of chapter 3 have an immediate use: if a reducible representation splits as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>, the inner product of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>’s characters with row <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>. This is the <b>reduction formula</b>:"},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>h</mi></mfrac><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mtext>classes </mtext><mi>c</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mtext>  </mtext><msup><mi>χ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext>  </mtext><msup><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"h is the order of the group and g_c the number of elements in class c."},{"p":"The use is always three steps. <b>Step one: count characters.</b> For each class pick one operation and see what it does to the set. The rule is simple: objects moved elsewhere contribute 0; objects left in place contribute the multiple of themselves they turn into (an unmoved bond or s orbital gives 1, a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>z</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> turned upside down gives −1, the three displacements of an unmoved atom give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mi>R</mi></mrow></semantics></math>). <b>Step two: apply the formula.</b> Every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> must be a non-negative integer, a built-in check on the counting. <b>Step three: check dimensions</b>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> equals the number of objects."},{"demo":"reduce"},{"h":"Methane: why “sp³”"},{"p":"The characters of methane’s four C–H bonds in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>: under <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow></semantics></math> all four stay (4); <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> keeps only the bond on its axis (1); <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> keep none (0); <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> keeps the two in the plane (2). Reduction gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>. From the table, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> matches carbon’s 2s and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> matches <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>, the three 2p orbitals. So four equivalent bonds need s plus three p from carbon: that is “sp³ hybridisation” in the language of groups. It also explains why methane’s photoelectron spectrum has <b>two</b> valence bands (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>), not the single one four equivalent bonds might suggest."},{"h":"Projection operators: building symmetry-adapted combinations"},{"p":"Reduction tells you “how many”; the <b>projection operator</b> tells you “what they look like”. Take any one object <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> (say the 1s of the first H), apply every operation to it, multiply by the character of irrep <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and add: the result transforms as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> (if it is not zero):"},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>P</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>h</mi></mfrac><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></munder><msup><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>∗</mo></msup><mtext>  </mtext><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext> </mtext><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":""},{"p":"For ammonia’s three H 1s orbitals (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></semantics></math>): projecting onto <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>; projecting onto <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow></semantics></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mn>6</mn></msqrt></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>, and projecting from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> then orthogonalising gives the other half, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>. These <b>symmetry-adapted linear combinations</b> (SALCs) can mix only with nitrogen orbitals of the same symmetry: the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> one with N 2s and 2p_z, the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow></semantics></math> pair with N 2p_x and 2p_y. The secular determinant therefore falls into small blocks, and a 3 × 3 or larger problem becomes a few 1 × 1 and 2 × 2 ones. The “symmetry” option in quantum-chemistry programs does exactly this."},{"note":"<b>A common shortcut, and its limit.</b> For projection, is one operation per class enough? No: you must sum over <b>every</b> element of the group (different operations in one class send <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> to different places). The demo’s table shows only the class totals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><mi>χ</mi><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>χ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>; for projection the classes have to be taken apart.","kind":"warn"}],"points":[{"h":"The reduction formula","p":"nᵢ is the inner product of Γ with row i; it must be a non-negative integer.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Only what stays put contributes","p":"Objects moved away contribute 0; those left contribute the factor they are multiplied by (1, −1, tr R, …).","st":"proved"},{"h":"Projection operators","p":"P̂⁽ⁱ⁾φ sums over all elements and gives a combination transforming as Γ⁽ⁱ⁾.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Block-diagonal secular equations","p":"Hamiltonian matrix elements between different irreps vanish, so a big problem splits into small blocks.","st":"proved"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>h</mi></mfrac><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>c</mi></munder><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>χ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>dim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The reduction formula and the dimension check."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>P</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>h</mi></mfrac><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>g</mi></munder><msup><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>∗</mo></msup><mtext> </mtext><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msup><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>P</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><msup><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>P</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>P</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Projection operators are idempotent and mutually orthogonal."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>H</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"H is totally symmetric, so it connects only states of the same symmetry."}],"example":{"q":"What representation do the six S–F σ bonds of octahedral <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> form in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, and which sulfur orbitals can bond with them? (Classes: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>8</mn><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>6</mn><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>6</mn><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">=</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mn>4</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>6</mn><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>8</mn><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>6</mn><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>.)","steps":["Count unmoved bonds: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow></semantics></math> 6, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> 0, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> 0, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> 2 (the two on the axis), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mn>4</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></semantics></math> 2, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></semantics></math> 0, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> 0, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> 0, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> 4 (the four in the plane), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> 2.","Reduce: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>48</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>; likewise <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, the rest 0. Dimension check: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></semantics></math>.","From the table: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> is s, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>, i.e. p, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>, i.e. two d orbitals."],"a":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, so symmetry allows s + p³ + d² (“sp³d²”). Calculations show, however, that sulfur’s 3d orbitals contribute little, and SF₆ is held together mainly by ionic and multicentre bonding: group theory says what is allowed, not how much."},"cites":""},"orbitals":{"id":"orbitals","body":[{"p":"A molecule’s Hamiltonian is unchanged by every operation of its point group, so its eigenfunctions, the molecular orbitals, must transform as irreducible representations of the group (<a href=\"#c-what\" data-open=\"what\">01 · What a group is: the rules that symmetry operations obey</a>). Two consequences follow at once. First, <b>only atomic orbitals of the same symmetry can combine</b>: Hamiltonian matrix elements between different irreps vanish (<a href=\"#c-reduce\" data-open=\"reduce\">04 · The reduction formula and projection operators: sorting a set of objects by symmetry</a>). Second, <b>an orbital’s degeneracy is the dimension of its irrep</b>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow></semantics></math> orbitals come in pairs, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></semantics></math> orbitals in threes. The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, … in front of each orbital in a quantum-chemistry output are exactly these labels."},{"h":"Water"},{"p":"Water is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>. Oxygen’s valence orbitals: 2s and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>z</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> (perpendicular to the molecular plane), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>. The two H 1s orbitals form two SALCs, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> (same sign) and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> (opposite signs). So the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> block is 3 × 3 (O 2s, O 2p_z, the H <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> combination), the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> block 2 × 2, and the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> block contains only O <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, which finds no partner of its symmetry and must be <b>non-bonding</b>. Ordered by energy: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mtext> </mtext><mn>2</mn><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mtext> </mtext><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mtext> </mtext><mn>3</mn><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mtext> </mtext><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></semantics></math> (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> is O 1s), and the highest occupied orbital <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> is one of the familiar “lone pairs”. The photoelectron spectrum shows exactly four valence ionisation energies, in this order."},{"h":"Cyclic conjugated molecules: no equations to solve"},{"p":"The π system of a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> ring (one <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>z</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> per carbon) transforms under the rotations of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> is cyclic, with n one-dimensional irreps; the k-th multiplies by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> under a turn of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. So the molecular-orbital coefficients <b>must</b> be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math>, with no secular equation to solve. Put them into the Hückel model (only the resonance integral β between neighbouring carbons, Hückel 1931 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-huckel1931\" data-ref=\"huckel1931\">Hückel 1931</a>]</span>) and the energies follow at once:"},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>β</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>…</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"β &lt; 0, so k = 0 is lowest; k and −k are degenerate."},{"p":"Frost and Musulin noticed a mnemonic in 1953: inscribe a regular n-gon, vertex down, in a circle of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>β</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, and the height of each vertex is an energy level <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-frost1953\" data-ref=\"frost1953\">Frost &amp; Musulin 1953</a>]</span>. The picture shows at a glance: the lowest orbital is single, the rest come in degenerate pairs, and for even rings the highest is single too. 4N + 2 π electrons exactly fill a closed shell (benzene 6, cyclopentadienyl anion 6, tropylium 6, cyclopropenyl cation 2) and are especially stable: <b>aromaticity</b>. 4N electrons put one electron in each of the top degenerate pair, giving a diradical or a distortion (cyclobutadiene is rectangular): <b>antiaromaticity</b>."},{"demo":"huckel"},{"p":"Benzene’s point group is really <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>6</mn><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, not just <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>. In <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>6</mn><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> the six π orbitals are labelled <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> (k = 0), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> (k = ±1), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> (k = ±2) and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> (k = 3), which is exactly the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> that “benzene’s π system” reduced to in the chapter 4 demo. The lowest electronic excitation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> gives three excited states, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>; only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> transforms as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> and is dipole allowed, so benzene absorbs strongly near 180 nm, while the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> band near 254 nm is weak and seen only because vibrations “lend” it intensity."},{"h":"Symmetry in reactions: the Woodward–Hoffmann rules"},{"p":"If a reaction keeps some symmetry element all the way (a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> axis or a mirror in an electrocyclic reaction, say), each reactant orbital must turn <b>continuously</b> into a product orbital of the same symmetry. In 1965 Woodward and Hoffmann used this “conservation of orbital symmetry” to explain a long-standing puzzle: when butadiene closes to cyclobutene, the ends turn the same way (conrotatory) on heating and opposite ways (disrotatory) under light <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-woodward1965\" data-ref=\"woodward1965\">Woodward &amp; Hoffmann 1965</a>]</span>. On heating the reaction runs on the ground state, and only conrotation connects occupied orbitals continuously to occupied ones; light promotes an electron and reverses the conclusion. It is a famous example of symmetry going from a classification tool to a predictive one, and Hoffmann shared the 1981 Nobel Prize in Chemistry with Kenichi Fukui for it."},{"note":"<b>Selection rules are the same idea.</b> In atoms or centrosymmetric molecules the electric-dipole operator <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is u, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></semantics></math> transitions are forbidden and only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>↔</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is allowed: the Laporte rule <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-laporte1925\" data-ref=\"laporte1925\">Laporte &amp; Meggers 1925</a>]</span>. The d–d transitions of octahedral complexes are all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, hence weak (molar absorptivities often only tens) and pale in colour (<a href=\"#c-ligand\" data-open=\"ligand\">07 · Ligand fields: why and how d orbitals split</a>).","kind":"info"}],"points":[{"h":"Orbitals are labelled by irreps","p":"a₁, b₂, e_g, … are orbital symmetries; degeneracy equals the irrep’s dimension.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Only like symmetries mix","p":"An atomic orbital with no partner of its symmetry is non-bonding (water’s 1b₁).","st":"proved"},{"h":"Cyclic π systems","p":"Cₙ symmetry gives the coefficients e^{2πikj/n} directly; the Hückel model gives α + 2β cos(2πk/n).","st":"approx"},{"h":"The 4N + 2 rule","p":"4N + 2 π electrons close a shell (aromatic); 4N give an open shell or a distortion (antiaromatic).","st":"tested"},{"h":"Conservation of orbital symmetry","p":"Along a reaction that keeps a symmetry element, orbitals connect continuously to orbitals of the same symmetry; it predicted opposite stereochemistry under heat and light.","st":"tested"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt></mfrac><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>β</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Orbitals and energies of a cyclic π system (Hückel)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext>  </mtext><mo>⇒</mo><mtext>  </mtext><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>⊗</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi></msub><mo>⊗</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The selection rule for electric-dipole transitions: with no totally symmetric part in the product, the transition is forbidden."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>β</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>deloc</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>β</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>β</mi></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Benzene’s π energy and delocalisation energy (relative to three isolated double bonds)."}],"example":{"q":"Using the Frost circle, which of the cyclopentadienyl cation, radical and anion <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> is aromatic?","steps":["n = 5: levels at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>β</mi></mrow></semantics></math> (one), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>0.618</mn><mi>β</mi></mrow></semantics></math> (two) and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1.618</mn><mi>β</mi></mrow></semantics></math> (two).","The three have 4, 5 and 6 π electrons. Six fill the lowest orbital and the degenerate bonding pair, a closed shell; four and five leave the degenerate pair unfilled."],"a":"The anion (6 π electrons, 4N + 2 with N = 1) is aromatic, which is why cyclopentadiene’s C–H is unusually acidic (pKa about 16) and why ferrocene contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>5</mn><mo>−</mo></msubsup></mrow></semantics></math>. Pick “Cyclopentadienyl anion” in the demo, then change the electron count to 4."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-laporte1925\" data-ref=\"laporte1925\">Laporte &amp; Meggers 1925</a> · <a href=\"#ref-huckel1931\" data-ref=\"huckel1931\">Hückel 1931</a> · <a href=\"#ref-frost1953\" data-ref=\"frost1953\">Frost &amp; Musulin 1953</a> · <a href=\"#ref-woodward1965\" data-ref=\"woodward1965\">Woodward &amp; Hoffmann 1965</a>"},"vibrations":{"id":"vibrations","body":[{"p":"Each of N atoms has three displacements x, y, z: 3N degrees of freedom forming a 3N-dimensional representation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> of the point group. Three are overall translations and three overall rotations (two for a linear molecule), leaving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></semantics></math> vibrations. In the harmonic approximation the vibrations separate into uncoupled <b>normal modes</b>, each transforming as one irreducible representation: the potential energy is unchanged by symmetry operations, so the Hessian commutes with each of them (<a href=\"#c-what\" data-open=\"what\">01 · What a group is: the rules that symmetry operations obey</a>)."},{"h":"Γvib in three steps"},{"steps":["<b>Count the atoms that stay put.</b> For each class pick one operation and count the atoms left in place. An atom moved elsewhere takes its three displacements with it and contributes nothing to the trace.","<b>Multiply by each unmoved atom’s contribution.</b> An unmoved atom’s three displacements transform by the 3 × 3 matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and contribute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mi>R</mi></mrow></semantics></math>: 3 for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, 1 for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, −3 for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>. The product is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>.","<b>Subtract translations and rotations, then reduce.</b> Translations transform as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> and rotations as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>, both read straight from the right-hand columns of the character table. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mtext>vib</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mtext>trans</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mtext>rot</mtext></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, split with the reduction formula (<a href=\"#c-reduce\" data-open=\"reduce\">04 · The reduction formula and projection operators: sorting a set of objects by symmetry</a>)."]},{"p":"Take water (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, molecule in the yz plane): the numbers of unmoved atoms are 3, 1, 1, 3 (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>v</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>), each contributes 3, −1, 1, 1, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>9</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>; subtract translations and rotations and reduce to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mtext>vib</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>: the symmetric stretch and the bend (both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>) and the antisymmetric stretch (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>). In the demo, click each column header to see which atoms stay put."},{"demo":"vib"},{"h":"IR, Raman and mutual exclusion"},{"p":"A vibrational fundamental absorbs in the <b>infrared</b> if the vibration changes the dipole moment; in group-theory terms, if the mode’s irrep is that of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></semantics></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. It scatters in <b>Raman</b> if it changes the polarisability: if its irrep is that of some quadratic function (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, …). Both are only “allowed by symmetry”; intensities depend on the actual electronic structure. Modes satisfying neither are <b>silent</b> and absent from fundamental spectra (though they may appear in combination bands, in neutron scattering or in environments that break the symmetry)."},{"p":"In a centrosymmetric molecule <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></semantics></math> all change sign under inversion (u) and quadratic functions do not (g), so no mode can be both IR and Raman active: the <b>mutual-exclusion rule</b>. The symmetric stretch of CO₂ (near 1388 cm⁻¹, in Fermi resonance with the bending overtone) appears only in Raman, the antisymmetric stretch (2349 cm⁻¹) only in IR. Conversely, if several IR and Raman bands coincide, the molecule very likely has no centre of symmetry. Before X-ray structures, this is how chemists decided molecular shapes."},{"h":"Solving it for real: symmetry appears by itself"},{"p":"The analysis above used no force constants at all. To see how it relates to a “real calculation”, the demo below writes down the simplest valence force field (bond stretches plus angle bends), diagonalises the mass-weighted Hessian and obtains all the normal modes; it then applies every symmetry operation to each mode, measures its character and matches a row of the character table. The program is never told the molecule’s symmetry, yet the modes come out grouped by irreps, and degenerate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></semantics></math> modes have exactly equal frequencies. The force constants are rough, so frequencies are 5–20 % off experiment, but the symmetry species and degeneracies are exact: the numbers depend on the model, the shape does not. Wilson used this kind of analysis in 1934 to number benzene’s 20 kinds of vibration <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-wilson1934\" data-ref=\"wilson1934\">Wilson 1934</a>]</span>, and later developed the GF matrix method that solves the Hessian block by symmetry block <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-wilson1939\" data-ref=\"wilson1939\">Wilson 1939</a>]</span>. Experimental frequencies are from Shimanouchi’s tables <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-shimanouchi1972\" data-ref=\"shimanouchi1972\">Shimanouchi 1972</a>]</span>."},{"demo":"modes"},{"note":"<b>See real spectra in the Spectra hall.</b> The <a href=\"../spectra/#m-ir\">infrared</a> and <a href=\"../spectra/#m-raman\">Raman</a> pages have measured spectra and spectra computed with PySCF, to compare with the band counts found here.","kind":"info"}],"points":[{"h":"3N − 6 vibrations","p":"Γvib = Γ3N − Γtrans − Γrot; 3N − 5 for linear molecules.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Count only atoms that stay put","p":"χ3N(R) = number of unmoved atoms × tr R.","st":"proved"},{"h":"IR and Raman selection rules","p":"IR: same symmetry as x, y, z; Raman: same as a quadratic function. They say “allowed”, not how strong.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Mutual exclusion","p":"With a centre of symmetry, no fundamental is both IR and Raman active.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Harmonic approximation","p":"Normal modes and selection rules are harmonic-approximation results; anharmonicity brings overtones, combinations and Fermi resonance.","st":"approx"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>unmoved</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>×</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The + sign is for proper rotations, − for improper ones (σ, i, Sₙ)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mtext>vib</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>z</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Subtract translations and rotations."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">F</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">L</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">G</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">L</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">Λ</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msubsup><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>ν</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Wilson’s GF method: F is the force-constant matrix and G the kinetic matrix; both block by symmetry."}],"example":{"q":"How many IR and how many Raman fundamentals does square-planar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>) have?","steps":["5 atoms, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></semantics></math> vibrations. Picking XeF₄ in the demo, the reduction gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mtext>vib</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> (dimensions 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 4 = 9).","From the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> table: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, so the IR-active modes are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, <b>3 bands</b>. Quadratic functions are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, so the Raman-active ones are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, <b>3 bands</b>.","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> is neither x, y, z nor a quadratic function: a silent mode."],"a":"3 IR bands, 3 Raman bands and one silent mode, with no band in both (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> is centrosymmetric). Were XeF₄ tetrahedral, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> would give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, with both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> modes in IR and Raman: the spectra distinguish the two shapes at a glance."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-wilson1934\" data-ref=\"wilson1934\">Wilson 1934</a> · <a href=\"#ref-wilson1939\" data-ref=\"wilson1939\">Wilson 1939</a> · <a href=\"#ref-shimanouchi1972\" data-ref=\"shimanouchi1972\">Shimanouchi 1972</a>"},"ligand":{"id":"ligand","body":[{"p":"A free atom is perfectly spherical, and its five d orbitals form the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></semantics></math> irrep of the rotation group: degenerate. Surround it with ligands and the symmetry drops to a point group, under which this five-dimensional representation is usually <b>reducible</b>. Bethe wrote down the group-theoretical solution in 1929 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bethe1929\" data-ref=\"bethe1929\">Bethe 1929</a>]</span>: the character of the d orbitals for a rotation by θ is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>θ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> (<a href=\"#c-angular\" data-open=\"angular\">10 · Angular momentum: representations of the rotation group, Clebsch–Gordan coefficients and the Wigner–Eckart theorem</a>), and +1 under inversion (d orbitals are g). Put these characters into the classes of the point group and reduce (<a href=\"#c-reduce\" data-open=\"reduce\">04 · The reduction formula and projection operators: sorting a set of objects by symmetry</a>): out come the sets."},{"table":{"head":["Geometry","Point group","d orbitals split into"],"rows":[["Octahedral","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>y</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>z</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>"],["Tetrahedral","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>y</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>z</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>, e below"],["Elongated octahedron","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>z</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>"],["Square planar","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>","The same four sets, with x² − y² far above the other four"],["Trigonal bipyramidal","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">′</mo><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">′</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>z</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>y</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>"]],"cap":"The number of sets and their degeneracies are fixed by symmetry; their order and spacing depend on the ligands (the usual order is given)."}},{"p":"Why is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> on top in an octahedron? Because the lobes of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> point straight at the ligands and are repelled most by their lone pairs (or, in molecular-orbital language, form antibonding combinations with the ligand σ orbitals); the lobes of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> lie between the ligands. The gap is called <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, typically 10 000–30 000 cm⁻¹, right in the visible and near ultraviolet: that is why transition-metal complexes are coloured. In a tetrahedron the order flips, and with only four ligands none of which points at a d orbital, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>9</mn></mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>."},{"demo":"aom"},{"h":"The angular overlap model"},{"p":"The demo uses the <b>angular overlap model</b> (AOM, Schäffer and Jørgensen 1965 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-schaffer1965\" data-ref=\"schaffer1965\">Schäffer &amp; Jørgensen 1965</a>]</span>): each ligand interacts only with the d orbitals pointing at it, raising each by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo>×</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is that d orbital’s angular value in the ligand’s direction. It is closer to chemical intuition than the point-charge crystal field, and its parameters (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>π</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>) transfer between geometries. With σ interactions alone the octahedral <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> stays exactly put, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>; tetrahedral <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>9</mn></mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>. In this simplest version the <b>pattern</b> of the splitting is fixed entirely by symmetry, and the numbers come from the model. The earlier ligand-field theory goes back to Van Vleck, who unified the crystal-field and molecular-orbital pictures <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-vanvleck1932\" data-ref=\"vanvleck1932\">Van Vleck 1932</a>]</span>."},{"h":"High spin and low spin"},{"p":"In an octahedron <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>–<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>7</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> can be filled in two ways: fill <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> before <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> (<b>low spin</b>, paying extra pairing energy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow></semantics></math>), or spread out first by Hund’s rule (<b>high spin</b>, paying extra <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>). Low spin wins when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. Strong-field ligands (CN⁻, CO) give large <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and weak-field ligands (I⁻, Br⁻, H₂O) small; the ordering is the spectrochemical series. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> is low-spin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> and diamagnetic; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> is high spin with four unpaired electrons. Drag the pairing energy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow></semantics></math> in the demo and watch the ground state jump between the two."},{"p":"With several electrons it is more complicated: each free-ion term (say ³F, ³P, ¹D, … for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>) splits in its own way in an octahedral field, and plotting the levels against <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow></semantics></math> gives the <b>Tanabe–Sugano diagrams</b> <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-tanabe1954\" data-ref=\"tanabe1954\">Tanabe &amp; Sugano 1954</a>]</span> used to assign every d–d band in a complex’s absorption spectrum. Every line’s label (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></msup><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></msup><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, …) is an irrep of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>."},{"h":"The Jahn–Teller effect: degeneracy breaks itself"},{"p":"In 1937 Jahn and Teller proved with group theory that a non-linear molecule in an <b>orbitally degenerate</b> electronic state always has some distortion that splits the degeneracy and lowers the energy <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-jahnteller1937\" data-ref=\"jahnteller1937\">Jahn &amp; Teller 1937</a>]</span>. The key: the direct product of a degenerate state with itself always contains some non-totally-symmetric vibrational irrep, so the energy has a first-order term in that distortion coordinate, and the symmetric geometry cannot be a minimum. The most famous example is Cu(II) (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>9</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>): three electrons in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, degenerate; elongate the octahedron along z and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> drops while <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> rises, with “two electrons below, one above” for a net gain. Nearly every six-coordinate Cu(II) compound is an elongated octahedron. Pick “Jahn–Teller elongation of Cu(II) d⁹” in the demo."},{"note":"<b>Why the colours are so pale.</b> Octahedral d–d transitions are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, forbidden by the Laporte rule (<a href=\"#c-orbitals\" data-open=\"orbitals\">05 · Molecular orbitals: symmetry fixes the shape of the answer first</a>); they borrow a little intensity only when vibrations momentarily break the centre of symmetry, so molar absorptivities are often just 1–100. Tetrahedral complexes, lacking a centre (such as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>), are far more intensely coloured. See more examples in the <a href=\"../chemistry/coordination/\">Coordination chemistry</a> hall.","kind":"info"}],"points":[{"h":"The splitting pattern is a theorem","p":"Which irreps the d orbitals reduce to depends only on the point group: in Oh it is always T₂g + E_g.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Order and spacing need a model","p":"Crystal-field, angular-overlap and ligand-field models give Δ; first-principles calculations give better numbers.","st":"approx"},{"h":"High and low spin","p":"Whether Δ or the pairing energy P is larger decides the ground state; the spectrochemical series ranks ligand field strength.","st":"tested"},{"h":"The Jahn–Teller theorem","p":"An orbitally degenerate ground state of a non-linear molecule is unstable to some symmetry-lowering distortion.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Descent in symmetry","p":"When symmetry drops, irreps of the larger group split into irreps of the subgroup by correlation tables (E_g → a₁g + b₁g).","st":"proved"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>:</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Characters of the d orbitals under rotations."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Splitting in octahedral and tetrahedral fields."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>L</mi></munder><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The σ-only angular overlap model."}],"example":{"q":"What is the crystal-field stabilisation energy of low-spin octahedral <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> (for example <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>)? When is low spin more stable than high spin?","steps":["With the barycentre as zero, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> sits at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.4</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.6</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>.","Low spin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow><mn>6</mn></msubsup></mrow></semantics></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>×</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.4</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2.4</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> with three pairs. High spin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow><mn>4</mn></msubsup><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></semantics></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>×</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>0.6</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.4</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> with one pair.","Low spin has two extra pairs, so the difference is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2.0</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>P</mi></mrow></semantics></math>: low spin is more stable when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></semantics></math>."],"a":"CFSE <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2.4</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> (plus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>P</mi></mrow></semantics></math> extra pairing energy); low spin when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. The demo’s AOM uses units of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>): pick “Octahedral d⁶” and drag <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow></semantics></math> from 1.5 past 3.5 to watch the ground state flip to high spin at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-bethe1929\" data-ref=\"bethe1929\">Bethe 1929</a> · <a href=\"#ref-vanvleck1932\" data-ref=\"vanvleck1932\">Van Vleck 1932</a> · <a href=\"#ref-jahnteller1937\" data-ref=\"jahnteller1937\">Jahn &amp; Teller 1937</a> · <a href=\"#ref-tanabe1954\" data-ref=\"tanabe1954\">Tanabe &amp; Sugano 1954</a> · <a href=\"#ref-schaffer1965\" data-ref=\"schaffer1965\">Schäffer &amp; Jørgensen 1965</a>"},"crystals":{"id":"crystals","body":[{"p":"An ideal crystal is one motif repeated without end in three directions. The translations <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">t</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> (integer <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>) that move each copy onto the next form an infinite abelian group, whose points are the <b>lattice</b>. The full set of a crystal’s symmetry operations is its <b>space group</b>, whose general element is written <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>R</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow></semantics></math>: a point operation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow></semantics></math> followed by a translation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">t</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. Unlike in molecules, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">t</mi></mrow></semantics></math> may be a fraction of a lattice translation: a half-turn followed by half a cell along the axis is a <b>two-fold screw axis</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, and a reflection followed by half a cell along the plane is a <b>glide plane</b>. They have no molecular counterpart, yet they are the commonest symmetry elements in crystals (the commonest space group of organic molecular crystals, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><msub><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, has both, and accounts for roughly a third of them)."},{"table":{"head":["Level","Number","What it counts"],"rows":[["Crystal systems","7","Lattice shapes: triclinic, monoclinic, orthorhombic, tetragonal, trigonal, hexagonal, cubic"],["Bravais lattices","14","Adding centring (body I, face F, base C)"],["Crystallographic point groups","32","Point groups with only 1-, 2-, 3-, 4-, 6-fold axes; they decide macroscopic properties (piezo- and pyroelectricity, optical activity)"],["Space groups","230","Adding screw axes and glides; listed completely and independently by Fedorov and Schoenflies in 1891"],["Plane: wallpaper groups","17","Two-dimensional space groups (Fedorov 1891, Pólya 1924)"]],"cap":"In three dimensions, counting each enantiomorphic pair once gives 219 space groups."}},{"h":"The seventeen wallpaper groups"},{"p":"In the plane there are only five lattices (oblique, rectangular, centred rectangular, square, hexagonal), the point operations are rotations and mirrors, and with glides they combine into exactly 17 repeating patterns. Pólya drew them all on one page in 1924 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-polya1924\" data-ref=\"polya1924\">Pólya 1924</a>]</span>, and Escher is said to have learned to design tilings from that page. Most of them can be found in the tilework of the Alhambra. The demo below uses an asymmetric “flag” as the motif, copies it with every operation of a group and tiles the plane: flags made by the same operation share a colour."},{"demo":"wallpaper"},{"p":"Reading the names (Hermann–Mauguin symbols): p is a primitive lattice and c a centred one; then come the order of the principal axis and, direction by direction, mirrors m and glides g. p4gm has a four-fold axis, a glide along one set of directions and a mirror along another. Three-dimensional space-group symbols read the same way, e.g. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>m</mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mn>3</mn><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></mrow></semantics></math> (NaCl, Cu), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><msub><mn>6</mn><mn>3</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></semantics></math> (Mg, graphite), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>d</mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mn>3</mn><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></mrow></semantics></math> (diamond, silicon)."},{"h":"The crystallographic restriction"},{"p":"The rotations of a periodic crystal can only be 1-, 2-, 3-, 4- or 6-fold. The proof takes two lines: in the lattice basis, a rotation that sends lattice points to lattice points has integer entries, so its trace is an integer; changing to a Cartesian basis is a similarity transformation that leaves the trace unchanged, equal to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> in 2D). <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> can only be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>±</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></semantics></math>. Intuitively, regular pentagons cannot tile the plane."},{"demo":"restrict"},{"p":"So when Shechtman reported in 1984 that rapidly quenched Al–Mn alloys gave sharp diffraction spots with ten-fold (icosahedral) symmetry, many did not believe it <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-shechtman1984\" data-ref=\"shechtman1984\">Shechtman et al. 1984</a>]</span>. The resolution: such materials have <b>long-range orientational order</b> but no translational period, and are called <b>quasicrystals</b>. Their diffraction patterns can be understood as projections of a higher-dimensional (six-dimensional) periodic lattice into three dimensions. Shechtman received the 2011 Nobel Prize in Chemistry, and the International Union of Crystallography changed its definition of “crystal” from “periodic arrangement” to “a diffraction pattern consisting mainly of sharp spots”."},{"h":"How space groups are determined today"},{"p":"Experimentally, the space group is read from the symmetry of the diffraction pattern and from <b>systematic absences</b>: screw axes and glides make certain reflections cancel exactly (a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> axis, for instance, removes the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> spots with odd k); see <a href=\"../optics/#c-xray\">Optics · X-ray crystal diffraction</a>. Computationally, given atomic coordinates a program finds every symmetry operation within a tolerance and identifies the space group: materials databases and first-principles codes almost all use spglib <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-togo2024\" data-ref=\"togo2024\">Togo et al. 2024</a>]</span> (the Code Desk’s <a href=\"../code/#phonon\">crystal phonons</a> tab uses it to identify the space group before computing phonons); all of the International Tables data (general positions, Wyckoff positions, subgroup relations and irreps for every group) can be looked up and computed on the Bilbao Crystallographic Server <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-aroyo2006\" data-ref=\"aroyo2006\">Aroyo et al. 2006</a>]</span>. The wallpaper-group operations in this chapter’s demo are written from the general positions in International Tables volume A."}],"points":[{"h":"Space-group elements {R | t}","p":"A point operation plus a translation; t can be a fraction of a lattice vector, giving screw axes and glides.","st":"proved"},{"h":"7, 14, 32, 230","p":"The numbers of crystal systems, Bravais lattices, crystallographic point groups and space groups are theorems.","st":"proved"},{"h":"17 wallpaper groups","p":"Periodic patterns in the plane have exactly 17 kinds of symmetry.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Crystallographic restriction","p":"tr R is an integer in the lattice basis, so only n = 1, 2, 3, 4, 6.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Quasicrystals","p":"Sharp diffraction with five- or ten-fold symmetry but no translational period; what stabilises them is still debated.","st":"tested"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>R</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">t</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>R</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>S</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>S</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">u</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Seitz notation and multiplication in a space group."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mtext>  </mtext><mo>⟹</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The crystallographic restriction theorem."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></munder><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>l</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∥</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>:</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The structure factor and a systematic absence caused by a screw axis."}],"example":{"q":"Why does a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> screw axis along b remove the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> reflections with odd k?","steps":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> sends an atom at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>, so atoms come in pairs with the same scattering factor.","For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> only the y coordinate enters the phase: one pair contributes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>.","For odd k, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math> and the bracket vanishes; every pair cancels and the whole <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math>."],"a":"The screw axis offsets each pair by half a cell along b, so odd orders cancel exactly. Crystallographers read screw axes and glides from such absence patterns and narrow the space group down to a few candidates."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-polya1924\" data-ref=\"polya1924\">Pólya 1924</a> · <a href=\"#ref-shechtman1984\" data-ref=\"shechtman1984\">Shechtman et al. 1984</a> · <a href=\"#ref-aroyo2006\" data-ref=\"aroyo2006\">Aroyo et al. 2006</a> · <a href=\"#ref-togo2024\" data-ref=\"togo2024\">Togo et al. 2024</a>"},"lie":{"id":"lie","body":[{"p":"A point group has finitely many elements, but the elements of the rotation group SO(3) depend continuously on three parameters (two for the axis direction, one for the angle). Such a group is a <b>Lie group</b>, after Sophus Lie. The key idea is that every rotation can be built from a string of “barely turning” ones, so knowing the neighbourhood of the identity is enough. Turn by a small angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi></mrow></semantics></math> about z: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>ε</mi><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>z</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>z</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> is a <b>generator</b>; a finite rotation is its exponential:"},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">n</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>θ</mi><mtext> </mtext><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">n</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">J</mi></mrow></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":""},{"p":"The commutation relations are not an extra assumption: they come from rotations themselves not commuting. Turning about x then y differs from the reverse order by a small turn about z. The generators and their commutators make up the <b>Lie algebra</b>. Angular-momentum operators in quantum mechanics obey exactly these relations (with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>), so “angular momentum” and “generators of the rotation group” are the same thing; by Noether’s theorem rotational symmetry gives conservation of angular momentum <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-noether1918\" data-ref=\"noether1918\">Noether 1918</a>]</span>. From the three relations alone, pure algebra shows that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> has eigenvalues <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>z</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></semantics></math> may be an integer <b>or a half-integer</b>."},{"h":"Spin ½ and SU(2)"},{"p":"Where do half-integers come from? SO(3), the group of 3 × 3 rotation matrices, only accommodates integer <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></semantics></math>; half-integers belong to another group, <b>SU(2)</b>: 2 × 2 unitary matrices with determinant 1. Its Lie algebra is identical to that of SO(3) (the generators are half the Pauli matrices), but globally it differs: every three-dimensional rotation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow></semantics></math> corresponds to <b>two</b> SU(2) matrices <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. Turn the angle about an axis from 0 to 2π and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow></semantics></math> returns to the identity while <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi></mrow></semantics></math> arrives at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>; only at 4π does it come back to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>. Mathematically, SU(2) is the double cover of SO(3)."},{"demo":"su2"},{"p":"The minus sign is not a mathematical game. In 1975 two neutron-interferometry experiments split a neutron beam in two, let the spins in one path precess in a magnetic field, and recombined the paths: the interference fringes repeated every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow></semantics></math> of precession, not <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, directly showing the sign change after 360° <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-rauch1975\" data-ref=\"rauch1975\">Rauch et al. 1975</a>; <a href=\"#ref-werner1975\" data-ref=\"werner1975\">Werner et al. 1975</a>]</span>. It shows up in chemistry too: electrons have spin ½, so molecules with an odd number of electrons need the “double groups” of the point groups to classify levels once spin–orbit coupling is included."},{"h":"The classification of simple Lie algebras"},{"p":"From 1888 Killing set out to study every possible Lie algebra systematically <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-killing1888\" data-ref=\"killing1888\">Killing 1888</a>]</span>, and Cartan completed the classification in 1894: the complex simple Lie algebras are just four infinite families, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> (SU(n+1)), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> (SO(2n+1)), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> (Sp(2n)) and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> (SO(2n)), plus five exceptions, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>8</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>. It is one of the most beautiful classification theorems in mathematics, and nearly everything physics uses is in it: SU(2) for rotations, SU(3) for the strong interaction, SO(3,1) for the Lorentz group, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>8</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> in string theory and grand unification."},{"h":"SU(3) and the eightfold way"},{"p":"In the 1950s accelerators turned up dozens of hadrons with no apparent order. In 1961 Ne’eman <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-neeman1961\" data-ref=\"neeman1961\">Ne’eman 1961</a>]</span> and Gell-Mann <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-gellmann1962\" data-ref=\"gellmann1962\">Gell-Mann 1962</a>]</span> independently noticed that, laid out by isospin and strangeness, they fill exactly the 8- and 10-dimensional irreps of SU(3) (the “eightfold way”). One slot of the 10 was empty, and Gell-Mann predicted a particle with charge −1, strangeness −3 and mass near 1680 MeV, the Ω⁻; it was found at Brookhaven in 1964 with mass 1672 MeV. That same year Gell-Mann proposed that these representations come from a more basic 3-dimensional one: quarks <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-gellmann1964\" data-ref=\"gellmann1964\">Gell-Mann 1964</a>]</span>. Today’s Standard Model is a Yang–Mills theory on the gauge group SU(3) × SU(2) × U(1) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-yangmills1954\" data-ref=\"yangmills1954\">Yang &amp; Mills 1954</a>]</span>."}],"points":[{"h":"Generators and the Lie algebra","p":"R = e^{−iθ n·J}; near the identity a Lie group is determined by its generators’ commutators.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Angular momentum generates rotations","p":"[J_x, J_y] = iJ_z gives j(j+1) and m = −j…j, with j possibly half-integer.","st":"proved"},{"h":"SU(2) double-covers SO(3)","p":"Each rotation corresponds to ±U; a spin ½ changes sign after 2π and returns after 4π.","st":"proved"},{"h":"The 4π period is measured","p":"Two neutron-interferometry experiments in 1975.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Classification of simple Lie algebras","p":"Four infinite families plus five exceptions (Killing–Cartan).","st":"proved"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">n</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><mn mathvariant=\"double-struck\">1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">n</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">σ</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">n</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn mathvariant=\"double-struck\">1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"An element of SU(2)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><mi>U</mi><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The two-to-one map from SU(2) to SO(3)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>j</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>j</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>J</mi><mo>±</mo></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>j</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt><mtext>  </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>j</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The angular-momentum spectrum, derived from the commutators alone."}],"example":{"q":"What does a rotation by θ about z do to the spin-up state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mo>↑</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo></mrow></semantics></math>? And at 2π and 4π?","steps":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>z</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mo>↑</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mo>↑</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo></mrow></semantics></math>.","So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mo>↑</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mo>↑</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo></mrow></semantics></math>: the state is unchanged apart from a phase.","At θ = 2π the phase is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>π</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>; at θ = 4π it is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>."],"a":"An overall phase of a single state cannot be measured, but interfere a rotated part with an unrotated one and the minus sign becomes measurable cancellation: exactly what the neutron interferometers did. The right-hand figure in the demo is the path of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><mo>↑</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>U</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>↑</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo></mrow></semantics></math> in the complex plane."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-killing1888\" data-ref=\"killing1888\">Killing 1888</a> · <a href=\"#ref-noether1918\" data-ref=\"noether1918\">Noether 1918</a> · <a href=\"#ref-yangmills1954\" data-ref=\"yangmills1954\">Yang &amp; Mills 1954</a> · <a href=\"#ref-neeman1961\" data-ref=\"neeman1961\">Ne’eman 1961</a> · <a href=\"#ref-gellmann1962\" data-ref=\"gellmann1962\">Gell-Mann 1962</a> · <a href=\"#ref-gellmann1964\" data-ref=\"gellmann1964\">Gell-Mann 1964</a> · <a href=\"#ref-rauch1975\" data-ref=\"rauch1975\">Rauch et al. 1975</a> · <a href=\"#ref-werner1975\" data-ref=\"werner1975\">Werner et al. 1975</a>"},"angular":{"id":"angular","body":[{"p":"As chapter 9 said, irreps of the rotation group are labelled by one number <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></semantics></math> (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>…</mo></mrow></semantics></math>), have dimension <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math> and are written <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow></semantics></math>. For integer <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi></mrow></semantics></math> the basis is the spherical harmonics <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>: the angular parts of s, p, d and f orbitals are exactly the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></semantics></math> representations. For a rotation by θ the character depends only on θ (rotations by the same angle are conjugate):"},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>j</mi></munderover><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":""},{"p":"This is the formula Bethe used in chapter 7 to find how d orbitals split in a crystal field (<a href=\"#c-ligand\" data-open=\"ligand\">07 · Ligand fields: why and how d orbitals split</a>): put <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> into each class of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and reduce to get <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>. “Lowering the symmetry from a sphere to an octahedron” is, in group theory, <b>restricting</b> an SO(3) representation to the subgroup O and reducing."},{"h":"Adding two angular momenta"},{"p":"Two electrons; an electron’s orbit and its spin; a nucleus and an electron: put two angular momenta <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> together and the state space is the direct product <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo>⊗</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></mrow></semantics></math>, of dimension <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>. It is reducible, splitting into each J from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow></semantics></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> once (the Clebsch–Gordan series). One line with characters checks it: multiply the two characters and expand with the formula above. The change-of-basis coefficients are the <b>Clebsch–Gordan coefficients</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo></mrow></semantics></math>:"},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo>⊗</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></munderover><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>J</mi></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></munder><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":""},{"p":"CG coefficients depend only on the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></semantics></math>s and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></semantics></math>s, not on the particles or the Hamiltonian, so they can be computed once and tabulated. They form an orthogonal matrix (one block per M) and are usually taken real with the Condon–Shortley phase convention. The simplest example is two spins ½: the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math> triplet <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mo>↑</mo><mo>↑</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mo>↑</mo><mo>↓</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mo>↓</mo><mo>↑</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mo>↓</mo><mo>↓</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo></mrow></semantics></math> and the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math> singlet <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mo>↑</mo><mo>↓</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mo>↓</mo><mo>↑</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>. Racah gave the general closed formula in 1942 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-racah1942\" data-ref=\"racah1942\">Racah 1942</a>]</span>, and the demo uses it live."},{"demo":"cg"},{"h":"The Wigner–Eckart theorem"},{"p":"Operators can also be sorted by representations of the rotation group: scalars (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, like the Hamiltonian), vectors (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, like the dipole <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi></mrow></semantics></math>), rank-2 tensors (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, like the quadrupole moment), … A set of operators transforming as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is a rank-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></semantics></math> <b>irreducible tensor operator</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></semantics></math>. Wigner and Eckart proved <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-eckart1930\" data-ref=\"eckart1930\">Eckart 1930</a>]</span> that its matrix elements between angular-momentum eigenstates equal a CG coefficient times a single number independent of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></semantics></math> (the reduced matrix element):"},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><msup><mi>m</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mtext> </mtext><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>m</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>k</mi><mi>q</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>j</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><msup><mi>m</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∥</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∥</mi><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>j</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":""},{"p":"Geometry (the CG coefficient) and physics (the reduced matrix element) are separated for good. Wherever the CG coefficient vanishes, the matrix element must vanish, and out come all the selection rules: electric-dipole (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>) transitions require <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math> (not <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math>) and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, and with parity this gives atoms’ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>. The relative strengths of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></semantics></math> components within a multiplet (say, the intensity ratios of Zeeman lines) are likewise set by CG coefficients alone, with no physical parameter at all."},{"note":"<b>The same mathematics in machine learning.</b> E(3)-equivariant neural networks (such as <a href=\"../nequip/#c-tensor\">NequIP</a>) split each atom’s features by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi></mrow></semantics></math> into scalars, vectors, rank-2 tensors, …; when two features are multiplied, CG coefficients split <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊗</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, so the network’s output is guaranteed to rotate correctly with the molecule (<a href=\"#c-beyond\" data-open=\"beyond\">12 · Symmetry breaking and group theory today</a>). The table in the demo holds exactly the coefficients these networks use in every layer.","kind":"info"}],"points":[{"h":"Irreps of the rotation group","p":"Labelled by j, dimension 2j + 1; for integer l the basis is the spherical harmonics.","st":"proved"},{"h":"The Clebsch–Gordan series","p":"D^{j₁} ⊗ D^{j₂} = D^{|j₁−j₂|} ⊕ … ⊕ D^{j₁+j₂}, each once.","st":"proved"},{"h":"CG coefficients are pure geometry","p":"Independent of the particles and interactions; they form an orthogonal matrix.","st":"proved"},{"h":"The Wigner–Eckart theorem","p":"Matrix element = CG coefficient × reduced matrix element; selection rules and intensity ratios within a multiplet follow.","st":"proved"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></munder><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>J</mi><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msup><mi>J</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><msup><mi>M</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>J</mi><msup><mi>J</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><msup><mi>M</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Orthogonality of CG coefficients."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>↛</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Electric-dipole selection rules."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></munderover><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>J</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Dimension check."}],"example":{"q":"A p electron (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>) couples its orbital angular momentum to its spin (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>). What can the total <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></semantics></math> be? How is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo></mrow></semantics></math> written in terms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo></mrow></semantics></math>?","steps":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>. Dimensions: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></semantics></math>.","Two states have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>↓</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>↑</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo></mrow></semantics></math>. In the demo choose <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math> and read the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math> column: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math>."],"a":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></msqrt><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>↓</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></msqrt><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>↑</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo></mrow></semantics></math>. These are the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> levels split by spin–orbit coupling: sodium’s D doublet (589.0 and 589.6 nm) is the transitions from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>s</mi></mrow></semantics></math>."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-eckart1930\" data-ref=\"eckart1930\">Eckart 1930</a> · <a href=\"#ref-racah1942\" data-ref=\"racah1942\">Racah 1942</a>"},"bands":{"id":"bands","body":[{"p":"A crystal’s translations commute with each other, so every irrep of the translation group is one-dimensional (<a href=\"#c-reps\" data-open=\"reps\">03 · Representations and character tables</a>): a translation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">t</mi></mrow></semantics></math> can only multiply an eigenfunction by a phase <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>. This is <b>Bloch’s theorem</b> <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bloch1929\" data-ref=\"bloch1929\">Bloch 1929</a>]</span>: an electron wavefunction in a crystal is a plane wave times a periodic function. The wavevector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is simply the label of a translation-group irrep; it is defined only up to a reciprocal-lattice vector (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">G</mi></mrow></semantics></math> give the same representation), so it suffices to look in one cell of k-space, the <b>first Brillouin zone</b>. At each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></mrow></semantics></math> there is a ladder of eigenvalues; joined up, they are the <b>bands</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>."},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">t</mi></mrow></msup><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi></mrow></msup><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":""},{"h":"Little groups: what symmetry survives at each k"},{"p":"Point operations turn <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></mrow></semantics></math> into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, generally sending one k point to another: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>, so the bands have the crystal’s symmetry across the zone. At high-symmetry points, however, some operations leave <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></mrow></semantics></math> fixed (or change it only by a reciprocal-lattice vector), and they form the <b>little group of k</b>. Its irreps give the degeneracies and labels of the levels at that k. Bouckaert, Smoluchowski and Wigner did this systematically in 1936 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bsw1936\" data-ref=\"bsw1936\">Bouckaert et al. 1936</a>]</span>, leaving the names still in use: Γ (zone centre), X, M, L, K, … and labels like <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>. A label on a band-structure plot tells you what the wavefunction looks like and whether bands may cross (bands of different little-group irreps may cross; bands of the same irrep “avoid crossing”)."},{"p":"The demo below is nearly free electrons on a square lattice. With no potential (the empty lattice) the bands are parabolas folded into the zone, heavily degenerate at high-symmetry points. Switch on the periodic potential and the degeneracy at X (the middle of a zone edge) splits, opening a gap of about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow></semantics></math>: the starting point of the difference between insulators and metals. But at M (the zone corner) the little group is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, which has a two-dimensional irrep <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, so one pair of levels cannot be split however V is tuned."},{"demo":"bands"},{"h":"Time reversal and spin"},{"p":"Time reversal is not an ordinary symmetry operation (it is antiunitary), but it too produces degeneracies. Herring gave the rules in 1937 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-herring1937\" data-ref=\"herring1937\">Herring 1937</a>]</span>: without spin, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>; with spin–orbit coupling, time reversal guarantees that in a system with an odd number of electrons every level is at least doubly degenerate (Kramers’ theorem). At the “time-reversal-invariant momenta” where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">G</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, these Kramers pairs are protected."},{"h":"Symmetry and topology"},{"p":"In 2005 Kane and Mele found that time-reversal-symmetric insulators fall into two classes by a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> index, and the non-trivial class must have conducting states on its edges <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kanemele2005\" data-ref=\"kanemele2005\">Kane &amp; Mele 2005</a>]</span>: topological insulators. In 2011 Liang Fu showed that a crystal’s point-group symmetry can also protect topological states (topological crystalline insulators) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-fu2011\" data-ref=\"fu2011\">Fu 2011</a>]</span>. In 2017 two lines of work took this to its conclusion: <b>topological quantum chemistry</b> compares the little-group irreps of bands at high-symmetry points with “every band that atomic orbitals can combine into”, and whatever cannot be assembled that way must be topological <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bradlyn2017\" data-ref=\"bradlyn2017\">Bradlyn et al. 2017</a>]</span>; <b>symmetry indicators</b> compute topological invariants directly from how many times each irrep appears at the high-symmetry points <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-po2017\" data-ref=\"po2017\">Po et al. 2017</a>]</span>. In 2019 three teams used these methods to screen tens of thousands of known materials and found thousands of topological candidates <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-vergniory2019\" data-ref=\"vergniory2019\">Vergniory et al. 2019</a>; <a href=\"#ref-tang2019\" data-ref=\"tang2019\">Tang et al. 2019</a>; <a href=\"#ref-zhang2019\" data-ref=\"zhang2019\">Zhang et al. 2019</a>]</span>. It is one of the most active uses of representation theory today."},{"note":"<b>Connection to quasiparticles.</b> Phonons, magnons and other quasiparticles obey Bloch’s theorem too, and their dispersions are labelled by little-group irreps; that is how to read the dispersion curves in <a href=\"../quasiparticles/\">Quasiparticles</a> and the <a href=\"../quasiparticles/phonons/\">Phonon hall</a>.","kind":"info"}],"points":[{"h":"Bloch’s theorem","p":"The translation group is abelian with one-dimensional irreps, labelled by k.","st":"proved"},{"h":"The little group of k","p":"Point operations that fix k; its irreps give the degeneracies and labels of levels.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Protected degeneracies","p":"When the little group has a multidimensional irrep, no symmetric potential can split that degeneracy.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Kramers degeneracy","p":"With an odd number of electrons and time reversal, every level is at least doubly degenerate.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Symmetry decides topology","p":"Topological quantum chemistry and symmetry indicators decide band topology from irreps at high-symmetry points; they detect only some topological phases.","st":"frontier"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">G</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">G</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">G</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">G</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">G</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">G</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">G</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The plane-wave expansion (units ħ²/2m = 1); the demo diagonalises it."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mo>±</mo></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>±</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"First-order perturbation theory at X: a gap of 2|V₁₀| (k in units of 2π/a)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>:</mo><mi>R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">G</mi><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The little group of k."}],"example":{"q":"In the empty square lattice, how degenerate is the lowest level at M, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>? And with a periodic potential?","steps":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">G</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> is smallest for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">G</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>, all equal to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>: four-fold degenerate.","These four plane waves form a four-dimensional representation of M’s little group <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, which reduces to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></semantics></math> (or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, depending on orientation).","The periodic potential transforms as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, so it can separate different irreps but not the two states inside <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow></semantics></math>."],"a":"Four-fold in the empty lattice; with a potential it becomes 1 + 2 + 1, with the middle pair (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow></semantics></math>) always degenerate. Tune <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>10</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>11</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> in the demo: the “Levels at M” readout always contains a ×2."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-bloch1929\" data-ref=\"bloch1929\">Bloch 1929</a> · <a href=\"#ref-bsw1936\" data-ref=\"bsw1936\">Bouckaert et al. 1936</a> · <a href=\"#ref-herring1937\" data-ref=\"herring1937\">Herring 1937</a> · <a href=\"#ref-kanemele2005\" data-ref=\"kanemele2005\">Kane &amp; Mele 2005</a> · <a href=\"#ref-fu2011\" data-ref=\"fu2011\">Fu 2011</a> · <a href=\"#ref-bradlyn2017\" data-ref=\"bradlyn2017\">Bradlyn et al. 2017</a> · <a href=\"#ref-po2017\" data-ref=\"po2017\">Po et al. 2017</a> · <a href=\"#ref-tang2019\" data-ref=\"tang2019\">Tang et al. 2019</a> · <a href=\"#ref-vergniory2019\" data-ref=\"vergniory2019\">Vergniory et al. 2019</a> · <a href=\"#ref-zhang2019\" data-ref=\"zhang2019\">Zhang et al. 2019</a>"},"beyond":{"id":"beyond","body":[{"p":"In the previous eleven chapters symmetry was always “kept”: whatever symmetry the Hamiltonian had, its eigenstates were sorted by it. But the lowest-energy state need not have the Hamiltonian’s full symmetry. A ferromagnet’s interactions treat every direction in space alike, yet below the Curie temperature the magnetisation picks one; water’s equations are unchanged by every translation and rotation, yet ice keeps only the symmetry of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><msub><mn>6</mn><mn>3</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. Symmetric equations, an asymmetric ground state: <b>spontaneous symmetry breaking</b>. In Anderson’s words, more is different: the collective behaviour of many particles contains things that simply do not exist at the level of a single particle <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-anderson1972\" data-ref=\"anderson1972\">Anderson 1972</a>]</span>."},{"h":"Landau theory"},{"p":"Landau’s idea: near a phase transition, expand the free energy in an <b>order parameter</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></semantics></math> (magnetisation, polarisation, the superconducting gap, …) and keep only the terms symmetry allows. If the symmetry is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>→</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, only even powers are allowed: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>. Above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> the minimum is at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math>; below, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math> becomes a maximum, two mirror-image minima <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> appear and the system must pick one. Which terms are allowed, whether the transition is first or second order, and which subgroup of the high-temperature group the low-temperature phase keeps are all decided by group theory."},{"demo":"landau"},{"h":"Goldstone and Higgs"},{"p":"If the broken symmetry is <b>continuous</b> (the magnetisation can point anywhere, not just ±), slowly rotating the order parameter along the degenerate directions costs no energy, so there must be an excitation whose energy goes to zero: <b>Goldstone’s theorem</b> <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-goldstone1961\" data-ref=\"goldstone1961\">Goldstone 1961</a>; <a href=\"#ref-gsw1962\" data-ref=\"gsw1962\">Goldstone et al. 1962</a>]</span>. In a ferromagnet it is the magnon; in a crystal (broken translational symmetry) the acoustic phonon; see <a href=\"../quasiparticles/\">Quasiparticles</a>. But if the broken symmetry is a <b>gauge</b> symmetry, the Goldstone mode is “eaten” by the gauge field and the gauge boson acquires a mass: the 1964 mechanism of Englert–Brout <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-englert1964\" data-ref=\"englert1964\">Englert &amp; Brout 1964</a>]</span> and Higgs <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-higgs1964\" data-ref=\"higgs1964\">Higgs 1964</a>]</span>, of which the photon’s “mass” in a superconductor (the Meissner effect) is the same story. The LHC discovered the Higgs boson in 2012."},{"h":"Symmetries nature breaks outright"},{"p":"Symmetry can also break not “spontaneously” but because the law itself is asymmetric. In 1956 Lee and Yang pointed out that parity conservation had never been tested in the weak interaction <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-leeyang1956\" data-ref=\"leeyang1956\">Lee &amp; Yang 1956</a>]</span>; the next year Wu used the β decay of polarised ⁶⁰Co nuclei to show that it is indeed violated: electrons are emitted preferentially opposite to the nuclear spin <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-wu1957\" data-ref=\"wu1957\">Wu et al. 1957</a>]</span>. The world in the mirror obeys different physical laws from ours."},{"h":"A particle is a representation of a group"},{"p":"In 1939 Wigner asked: what are the unitary irreducible representations of the symmetry group of special relativity (the Poincaré group of translations, rotations and Lorentz boosts)? The answer is classified by two numbers: mass and spin (helicity when massless) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-wigner1939\" data-ref=\"wigner1939\">Wigner 1939</a>]</span>. Ever since, physicists mean by “an elementary particle” an irreducible representation of the Poincaré group, with internal symmetries (SU(3) × SU(2) × U(1)) adding labels such as charge and colour (<a href=\"#c-lie\" data-open=\"lie\">09 · Lie groups: continuous symmetry, the rotation group and spin</a>). Gross summed it up: symmetry dictates interaction <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-gross1996\" data-ref=\"gross1996\">Gross 1996</a>]</span>."},{"h":"Frontier one: writing symmetry into neural networks"},{"p":"Convolutional networks work well because they are translation-equivariant: shift the image and the feature maps shift with it. In 2016 Cohen and Welling generalised the idea to arbitrary groups <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-cohen2016\" data-ref=\"cohen2016\">Cohen &amp; Welling 2016</a>]</span>; in 2018 tensor field networks <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-thomas2018\" data-ref=\"thomas2018\">Thomas et al. 2018</a>]</span> and 3D steerable CNNs <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-weiler2018\" data-ref=\"weiler2018\">Weiler et al. 2018</a>]</span> organised features by irreps of SO(3) (scalars, vectors, rank-2 tensors, …) and multiplied them between layers with Clebsch–Gordan coefficients (<a href=\"#c-angular\" data-open=\"angular\">10 · Angular momentum: representations of the rotation group, Clebsch–Gordan coefficients and the Wigner–Eckart theorem</a>). e3nn turned these operations into a general-purpose library <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-geiger2022\" data-ref=\"geiger2022\">Geiger &amp; Smidt 2022</a>]</span>, and the NequIP interatomic potential built on it reached high accuracy from only a few hundred training structures <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-batzner2022\" data-ref=\"batzner2022\">Batzner et al. 2022</a>]</span>; see the <a href=\"../nequip/\">NequIP section</a>. With symmetry hard-wired, the network does not have to “learn” rotational invariance from data. Whether that always beats data augmentation has become a contested question again in the era of large models."},{"h":"Frontier two: the Monster and moonshine"},{"p":"The largest sporadic simple group in the classification of finite simple groups (<a href=\"#c-what\" data-open=\"what\">01 · What a group is: the rules that symmetry operations obey</a>), the Monster, has about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>53</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> elements, and its smallest non-trivial irrep has dimension 196 883 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-griess1982\" data-ref=\"griess1982\">Griess 1982</a>]</span>. In 1978 McKay noticed an absurd-looking coincidence: the second coefficient in the expansion of the modular function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> from number theory is 196 884 = 196 883 + 1. Coincidences of this kind came to be called “monstrous moonshine”. In 1992 Borcherds proved the conjecture with vertex operator algebras and tools from string theory <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-borcherds1992\" data-ref=\"borcherds1992\">Borcherds 1992</a>]</span>, winning the 1998 Fields Medal. Why a finite group should be tied to modular functions, and whether deeper physics lies behind it, still has no complete answer."}],"points":[{"h":"Spontaneous symmetry breaking","p":"Symmetric equations, asymmetric ground state; the low-temperature phase keeps a subgroup of the high-temperature group.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Landau theory is mean-field","p":"β = ½; the true 3D Ising value is about 0.326, which needs the renormalisation group.","st":"approx"},{"h":"Goldstone’s theorem","p":"Breaking a continuous symmetry gives a gapless excitation; for a gauge symmetry it is eaten (the Higgs mechanism).","st":"proved"},{"h":"Parity violation","p":"The weak interaction breaks mirror symmetry outright (1957).","st":"tested"},{"h":"Equivariant neural networks","p":"Features organised by irreps and multiplied with CG coefficients; very data-efficient, though whether symmetry should always be hard-wired is debated.","st":"frontier"},{"h":"Why moonshine exists","p":"Proved, but why it holds remains open.","st":"open"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi><mi>m</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi></mrow></mfrac></msqrt><mtext>  </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Landau free energy and the order parameter."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">#</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mtext>Goldstone modes</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>dim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>dim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>H</mi><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mtext>relativistic</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The number of Goldstone modes when G breaks to H in relativistic systems (non-relativistic systems can have fewer, as ferromagnets do)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>744</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>196</mn><mtext> </mtext><mn>884</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>21</mn><mtext> </mtext><mn>493</mn><mtext> </mtext><mn>760</mn><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo>⋯</mo><mtext> </mtext><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Expansion of the modular function j: 196 884 = 196 883 + 1."}],"example":{"q":"In the Landau model <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>), what is the equilibrium <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow></semantics></math> at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>? Set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>.","steps":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, with non-zero solution <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></semantics></math>.","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow></semantics></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.25</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow></semantics></math>. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">′</mo><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">′</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math> is a maximum."],"a":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, with two equally deep minima at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>±</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow></semantics></math>. Press “Cold, no field” in the demo and the readout shows <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5000</mn></mrow></semantics></math>; add a small field and the system picks just one."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-wigner1939\" data-ref=\"wigner1939\">Wigner 1939</a> · <a href=\"#ref-leeyang1956\" data-ref=\"leeyang1956\">Lee &amp; Yang 1956</a> · <a href=\"#ref-wu1957\" data-ref=\"wu1957\">Wu et al. 1957</a> · <a href=\"#ref-goldstone1961\" data-ref=\"goldstone1961\">Goldstone 1961</a> · <a href=\"#ref-gsw1962\" data-ref=\"gsw1962\">Goldstone et al. 1962</a> · <a href=\"#ref-englert1964\" data-ref=\"englert1964\">Englert &amp; Brout 1964</a> · <a href=\"#ref-higgs1964\" data-ref=\"higgs1964\">Higgs 1964</a> · <a href=\"#ref-anderson1972\" data-ref=\"anderson1972\">Anderson 1972</a> · <a href=\"#ref-griess1982\" data-ref=\"griess1982\">Griess 1982</a> · <a href=\"#ref-borcherds1992\" data-ref=\"borcherds1992\">Borcherds 1992</a> · <a href=\"#ref-gross1996\" data-ref=\"gross1996\">Gross 1996</a> · <a href=\"#ref-cohen2016\" data-ref=\"cohen2016\">Cohen &amp; Welling 2016</a> · <a href=\"#ref-thomas2018\" data-ref=\"thomas2018\">Thomas et al. 2018</a> · <a href=\"#ref-weiler2018\" data-ref=\"weiler2018\">Weiler et al. 2018</a> · <a href=\"#ref-batzner2022\" data-ref=\"batzner2022\">Batzner et al. 2022</a> · <a href=\"#ref-geiger2022\" data-ref=\"geiger2022\">Geiger &amp; Smidt 2022</a>"}}};
